                             8087/TCP                              2m13s\nsearch                   ClusterIP      10.99.252.198    <none>                                     8082/TCP                              2m11s\nuser                     ClusterIP      10.99.64.1       <none>                                     8086/TCP                              2m5s\n', id='f2877d83-ad66-4671-8216-64cc5bf3e9cf', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bc84f4c44da680fe'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='237dd925-3e9b-44b8-8e43-ca399ba909e9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using kubectl get services, which gave a list of services in the hotel-reservation namespace. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty.\n\nLooking at the services, there\'s frontend, geo, profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, search, user, and some MongoDB and Memcached services. Also, there are Jaeger services for tracing. The user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults. The goal is to identify the root cause of any faults.\n\nSince the initial step was to list the services, the next logical step is to check the deployments and pods for each service to see their status. If any deployments are not in a healthy state, that could indicate a problem. So using kubectl get deployments and kubectl get pods would help check the deployment status and pod health.\n\nAlternatively, checking metrics with get_metrics could show if any services are underperforming. But since the user hasn\'t mentioned specific metrics, maybe starting with the deployments and pods is better. Also, looking at the services\' endpoints and ensuring they are accessible could be important. But the user might need to check if the services are actually running and if there are any errors in their logs.\n\nAnother approach is to check the dependency graph using get_dependency_graph to see if there are any broken dependencies between services. But first, it\'s essential to confirm the current state of the services. So, the next tool should be to get deployments and pods. Let me check the available tools again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run kubectl get deployments and kubectl get pods. So, the next step is to run those commands to check the deployment status and pod health.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: To assess the health of microservices, I need to check the deployment status (e.g., replicas, availability) and pod health (e.g., crash loops, restarts). Running "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation" will show deployment states, while "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will reveal pod statuses. This will help identify if any services are misconfigured, crashing, or not scaling properly.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 478, 'prompt_tokens': 2636, 'total_tokens': 3114, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-47e8-7293-a320-c312e6d0551f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2636, 'output_tokens': 478, 'total_tokens': 3114}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0cb97b3a-9570-48b1-9ac3-4c8a097252e3'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with multiple microservices. They\'ve already used the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the list of services in the namespace. Now, they want to check the deployments and pods to see if there are any issues.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out which tool to use next. The available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which can run kubectl commands. The user\'s reasoning was to check deployments and pods for health issues. So, the next step is to get the deployments and then the pods.\n\nBut wait, the user can only make one tool call at a time. The previous step was getting services, and now they need to get deployments. So, the next tool call should be to run "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation" to check the deployment statuses. That will show the number of replicas, available replicas, and any errors in the deployments. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they should check the pods directly. But deployments are a higher-level abstraction, so checking deployments first would give an overview. If deployments are healthy, then the pods under them should be okay. But if a deployment is not healthy, that could indicate issues like failed rollouts or scaling problems.\n\nSo, the correct next step is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation". That will provide the necessary information about the deployments. After that, if any deployments are problematic, they can then check the pods for more details. But for now, the immediate action is to get the deployments.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b078a2f689584eee', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 361, 'prompt_tokens': 3640, 'total_tokens': 4001, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-5e18-7341-87be-aa7377a2b0b7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b078a2f689584eee', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3640, 'output_tokens': 361, 'total_tokens': 4001}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\nconsul                   1/1     1            1           2m55s\nfrontend                 1/1     1            1           2m55s\ngeo                      1/1     1            1           2m54s\njaeger                   1/1     1            1           2m51s\nmemcached-profile        1/1     1            1           2m49s\nmemcached-rate           1/1     1            1           2m44s\nmemcached-reserve        1/1     1            1           2m33s\nmongodb-geo              0/1     1            0           2m53s\nmongodb-profile          0/1     1            0           2m47s\nmongodb-rate             0/1     1            0           2m42s\nmongodb-recommendation   0/1     1            0           2m38s\nmongodb-reservation      0/1     1            0           2m29s\nmongodb-user             0/1     1            0           2m21s\nprofile                  1/1     1            1           2m46s\nrate                     0/1     1            0           2m40s\nrecommendation           1/1     1            1           2m35s\nreservation              0/1     1            0           2m27s\nsearch                   1/1     1            1           2m24s\nuser                     1/1     1            1           2m18s\n', id='695aaca5-3b82-455f-b7d8-9f3ebc5330c2', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b078a2f689584eee'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8dc7f321-2785-4a29-8591-37ee87fcb449'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with multiple microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl to get services and deployments. The services list showed some MongoDB services like mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile, etc., all with 0/1 available. The deployments also show several MongoDB services with 0/1 available, meaning they\'re not running. \n\nThe next step is to check why those deployments aren\'t available. The user might need to look into the pods associated with those deployments. For example, the mongodb-geo deployment has 0 pods available. So, using kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation would show the status of each pod. If the pods are crashing or not starting, that could indicate issues like configuration errors, resource limits, or image problems.\n\nAlternatively, checking the logs of the non-available pods could provide more insight. But first, confirming the pod statuses is essential. If the pods are in a CrashLoopBackOff state or have errors, that\'s a good starting point. Also, looking at the events related to those pods might show why they\'re not running. The user might need to check the pod details with kubectl describe pod <pod-name> to get more information on the specific issues. \n\nAnother angle is checking the resource quotas or limits in the namespace. If there\'s a LimitRange or ResourceQuota, it might be restricting the resources allocated to the pods, causing them to fail. But first, verifying the pod statuses and their logs would be more direct. So, the next tool call should be to get the list of pods in the namespace to see their current status.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The deployments for mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile, mongodb-rate, mongodb-recommendation, mongodb-reservation, mongodb-user, and rate are not available (0/1). To diagnose the root cause, I need to check the status of individual pods associated with these deployments. Running "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will reveal if pods are crashing, pending, or in error states, which will guide further investigation into specific failures.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 444, 'prompt_tokens': 3239, 'total_tokens': 3683, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-76b0-7303-ac62-954bf0ac0e33-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3239, 'output_tokens': 444, 'total_tokens': 3683}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0ffd4886-e8e1-4da9-9ac4-5b22586539c8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their hotel reservation application, which has multiple microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl to get services and deployments. The deployments for several MongoDB services and the \'rate\' service are showing 0/1, meaning they\'re not available. The next step is to check the pods associated with these deployments to see why they\'re not running.\n\nThe user chose to run "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to get the pod statuses. This makes sense because if the deployments aren\'t available, the pods might be in a crash loop, pending, or have errors. By checking the pods, they can get more detailed information about what\'s going wrong. \n\nOnce they have the pod statuses, they can look for patterns. For example, if pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, it might indicate application errors or configuration issues. If they\'re Pending, there could be resource constraints or scheduling problems. The output from this command will help pinpoint which specific pods are failing and why, leading to the root cause of the deployment issues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8d6bf51f7c0ef378', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 258, 'prompt_tokens': 4246, 'total_tokens': 4504, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-8bda-7d73-8f66-7350e530823c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8d6bf51f7c0ef378', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4246, 'output_tokens': 258, 'total_tokens': 4504}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                     READY   STATUS              RESTARTS        AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-6s4xr                  1/1     Running             0               3m4s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-qcb4d                1/1     Running             0               3m4s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-s9b66                     1/1     Running             2 (2m11s ago)   3m3s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-hgczs                  1/1     Running             0               3m\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-hgpdc        1/1     Running             0               2m57s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-qlwq9           1/1     Running             0               2m52s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-8644q       1/1     Running             0               2m41s\nmongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl             0/1     ContainerCreating   0               38s\nmongodb-profile-76fb8d4b68-nl5tj         0/1     ContainerCreating   0               36s\nmongodb-rate-b9589b8d-n67gv              0/1     ContainerCreating   0               31s\nmongodb-recommendation-cf88b8cc4-4zglc   0/1     ContainerCreating   0               27s\nmongodb-reservation-58fdc8958-h8rbb      0/1     ContainerCreating   0               28s\nmongodb-user-799846599d-nf6z6            0/1     ContainerCreating   0               26s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-8vctx                 1/1     Running             2 (115s ago)    2m55s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-h6rfh                    1/1     Running             3 (42s ago)     2m49s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-hd8gm          1/1     Running             2 (90s ago)     2m44s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-6fqt5             1/1     Running             3 (38s ago)     2m36s\nsearch-5f668579c9-lgwr6                  1/1     Running             0               2m32s\nuser-6548d868cd-md9hd                    1/1     Running             2 (72s ago)     2m27s\nwrk2-job-dnjvw                           1/1     Running             0               46s\n', id='28b9e9bd-088c-4669-b341-71a191deee2d', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8d6bf51f7c0ef378'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='8e9d0c5b-c794-4f5f-9099-93ffc2dc8af3'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why some MongoDB deployments are not available. From the previous steps, we saw that deployments like mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile, etc., have 0/1 replicas available. The pods for these are in ContainerCreating state. That usually means the pods are still being created, but there\'s an issue preventing them from starting.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the containers aren\'t starting. The next logical step is to check the logs of these pods. If the pods are in ContainerCreating state, maybe there\'s a problem with the container image, permissions, or resource limits. Using kubectl logs could help see if there are any errors during the container startup.\n\nBut wait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. So I can run "kubectl logs" on the specific pods. However, the pods are still in ContainerCreating, so maybe the logs aren\'t available yet. Alternatively, maybe the pods are failing to start, and the logs would show the reason. Let me check the available commands again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows "kubectl logs" as a command. So I can try that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe checking the events for the pods would help. The "kubectl describe pod" command can show events that might indicate why the pod isn\'t starting. But the user hasn\'t used that yet. Wait, the available commands include "kubectl describe", so maybe using that on the affected pods would give more detailed information.\n\nBut the user\'s last step was getting the list of pods, and the next step is to check the logs or describe the pods. Since the pods are in ContainerCreating, perhaps the issue is with the container image not being accessible, or the pod\'s configuration (like volumes, environment variables) being incorrect. \n\nSo, the best approach is to first check the logs of the pods. If the logs aren\'t available yet, maybe the describe command would show the events. Let me proceed with checking the logs first. Let\'s pick one of the MongoDB pods, like mongodb-geo, and get its logs. If that doesn\'t work, then use describe.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The MongoDB pods (e.g., mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile) are in "ContainerCreating" state, indicating they are not yet ready. To diagnose the root cause, I need to inspect the logs of these pods to identify startup errors, missing dependencies, or configuration issues. Using "kubectl logs" on the specific pods will reveal any immediate container failures or errors during initialization.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 533, 'prompt_tokens': 4137, 'total_tokens': 4670, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-9a75-7aa3-9ead-50ab8f74b393-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4137, 'output_tokens': 533, 'total_tokens': 4670}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bbcf10c7-5b63-4959-8fac-2b5893e3ab36'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why several MongoDB pods are in the ContainerCreating state. From the previous steps, they\'ve checked the deployments and pods, and the MongoDB-related pods are not ready. The next logical step is to check the logs of those pods to see if there are any errors during startup.\n\nThe tool available is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which allows running kubectl commands. The command to get logs for a specific pod is "kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>". Since the pods are named like mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl, I need to target each of the MongoDB pods that are in ContainerCreating state. However, the user might not want to check each one individually. Alternatively, maybe there\'s a pattern or a common issue across these pods.\n\nWait, but the user\'s last step was to get the list of pods, and the response shows that the MongoDB pods are in ContainerCreating. The next step is to check their logs. However, the tool requires specifying the exact pod name. Since there are multiple MongoDB pods, maybe the user should check each one. But the assistant can only make one tool call at a time. So, perhaps start with one pod, like mongodb-geo, to see if there\'s an error. If that\'s not possible, maybe check the logs for all pods with a certain label or pattern. But the available command is "kubectl logs", which requires the pod name. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user can check the events for the pods to see if there are any issues. But the available tools don\'t include checking events. The user has to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. So, the best approach is to run "kubectl logs" on each of the MongoDB pods. However, since the assistant can only make one tool call per step, they might need to start with one pod. But the user might have multiple pods, so maybe the assistant should check the logs for all MongoDB pods. Wait, but the command can\'t be done in one go. So, the assistant should pick one pod first. Let\'s check the logs for mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl. If that\'s not possible, maybe check the logs for the pod that\'s been in ContainerCreating the longest. Alternatively, maybe the user can check the logs for all pods in the namespace, but that\'s not efficient. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user can check the logs for the pods that are in ContainerCreating. Since the assistant can only make one tool call, they should choose one pod to check. Let\'s go with mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl. So the command would be "kubectl logs mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation". That should retrieve the logs for that specific pod, which might show any errors during container creation. If there\'s an error, that would indicate the root cause, like a missing volume, configuration issue, or dependency problem. If not, the assistant can proceed to check other pods.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9380f9a2b9440695', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 703, 'prompt_tokens': 5131, 'total_tokens': 5834, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-b389-7792-91e1-c60b0fbad0f4-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9380f9a2b9440695', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5131, 'output_tokens': 703, 'total_tokens': 5834}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (BadRequest): container "hotel-reserv-geo-mongo" in pod "mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl" is waiting to start: ContainerCreating\n', id='9e1b986b-74e1-4d97-9ead-38aa87f83bfc', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9380f9a2b9440695'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='66508b1d-f654-4faa-a07b-6486ff7b2476')]]
