vation" will reveal if pods are crashing, pending, or in error states, which will guide further investigation into specific failures.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 444, 'prompt_tokens': 3239, 'total_tokens': 3683, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-76b0-7303-ac62-954bf0ac0e33-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3239, 'output_tokens': 444, 'total_tokens': 3683}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0ffd4886-e8e1-4da9-9ac4-5b22586539c8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their hotel reservation application, which has multiple microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl to get services and deployments. The deployments for several MongoDB services and the \'rate\' service are showing 0/1, meaning they\'re not available. The next step is to check the pods associated with these deployments to see why they\'re not running.\n\nThe user chose to run "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to get the pod statuses. This makes sense because if the deployments aren\'t available, the pods might be in a crash loop, pending, or have errors. By checking the pods, they can get more detailed information about what\'s going wrong. \n\nOnce they have the pod statuses, they can look for patterns. For example, if pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, it might indicate application errors or configuration issues. If they\'re Pending, there could be resource constraints or scheduling problems. The output from this command will help pinpoint which specific pods are failing and why, leading to the root cause of the deployment issues.\n</t