    8085/TCP                              2m22s\nreservation              ClusterIP      10.99.99.90      <none>                                     8087/TCP                              2m13s\nsearch                   ClusterIP      10.99.252.198    <none>                                     8082/TCP                              2m11s\nuser                     ClusterIP      10.99.64.1       <none>                                     8086/TCP                              2m5s\n', id='f2877d83-ad66-4671-8216-64cc5bf3e9cf', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bc84f4c44da680fe'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='237dd925-3e9b-44b8-8e43-ca399ba909e9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using kubectl get services, which gave a list of services in the hotel-reservation namespace. Now, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty.\n\nLooking at the services, there\'s frontend, geo, profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, search, user, and some MongoDB and Memcached services. Also, there are Jaeger services for tracing. The user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults. The goal is to identify the root cause of any faults.\n\nSince the initial step was to list the services, the next logical step is to check the deployments and pods for each service to see their status. If any deployments are not in a healthy state, that could indicate a problem. So using kubectl get deployments and kubectl get pods would help check the deployment status and pod health.\n\nAlternatively, checking metrics with get_metrics could show if any services are underperforming. But since the user hasn\'t mentioned specific metrics, maybe starting with the deployments and pods is better. Also, looking at the services\' endpoints and ensuring they are accessible could be important. But the user might need to check if the services are actually running and if there are any errors in their logs.\n\nAnother approach is to check the dependency graph using get_dependency_graph to see if there are any broken dependencies between services. But first, it\'s essential to confirm the current state of the services. So, the next tool should be to get deployments and pods. Let me check the available tools again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run kubectl get deployments and kubectl get pods. So, the next step is to run those commands to check the deployment status and pod health.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: To assess the health of microservices, I need to check the deployment status (e.g., replicas, availability) and pod health (e.g., crash loops, restarts). Running "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation" will show deployment states, while "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will reveal pod statuses. This will help identify if any services are misconfigured, crashing, or not scaling properly.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 478, 'prompt_tokens': 2636, 'total_tokens': 3114, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-47e8-7293-a320-c312e6d0551f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2636, 'output_tokens': 478, 'total_tokens': 3114}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0cb97b3a-9570-48b1-9ac3-4c8a097252e3'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with multiple microservices. They\'ve already used the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the list of services in the namespace. Now, they want to check the deployments and pods to see if there are any issues.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out which tool to use next. The available tools include exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, which can run kubectl commands. The user\'s reasoning was to check deployments and pods for health issues. So, the next step is to get the deployments and then the pods.\n\nBut wait, the user can only make one tool call at a time. The previous step was getting services, and now they need to get deployments. So, the next tool call should be to run "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation" to check the deployment statuses. That will show the number of replicas, available replicas, and any errors in the deployments. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they should check the pods directly. But deployments are a higher-level abstraction, so checking deployments first would give an overview. If deployments are healthy, then the pods under them should be okay. But if a deployment is not healthy, that could indicate issues like failed rollouts or scaling problems.\n\nSo, the correct next step is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation". That will provide the necessary information about the deployments. After that, if any deployments are problematic, they can then check the pods for more details. But for now, the immediate action is to get the deployments.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b078a2f689584eee', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 361, 'prompt_tokens': 3640, 'total_tokens': 4001, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_to