rets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-m8dpl (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type                        Status\n  PodReadyToStartContainers   False \n  Initialized                 True \n  Ready                       False \n  ContainersReady             False \n  PodScheduled                True \nVolumes:\n  geo:\n    Type:       PersistentVolumeClaim (a reference to a PersistentVolumeClaim in the same namespace)\n    ClaimName:  geo-pvc\n    ReadOnly:   false\n  init-script:\n    Type:      ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:      mongo-geo-script\n    Optional:  false\n  failure-script:\n    Type:      ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:      failure-admin-geo\n    Optional:  false\n  kube-api-access-m8dpl:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   Burstable\nNode-Selectors:              <none>\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason     Age   From               Message\n  ----    ------     ----  ----               -------\n  Normal  Scheduled  60s   default-scheduler  Successfully assigned hotel-reservation/mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl to node0.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Normal  Pulling    49s   kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-geo-mongo}: Pulling image "mongo:8.0.14-rc0"\n', id='5e20cb7e-d047-494e-8053-aafa2f3a130e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b02865cfbddc7fca'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='487f670a-63da-4fbe-8c8c-c03b2c7e8ceb'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the MongoDB geo pod is in a ContainerCreating state. From the previous steps, we know that the pod is pending, and the container is waiting to start. The describe pod output shows that the container is pulling the image "mongo:8.0.14-rc0". \n\nFirst, I need to check if the image pull is successful. The event mentions "Pulling image", but it doesn\'t say if it\'s completed. If the image pull fails, the pod won\'t start. So maybe the image isn\'t available in the registry, or there\'s a network issue preventing it from being pulled.\n\nAnother thing to check is the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) mentioned in the volumes section. The geo PVC is being used, and if that\'s not properly provisioned, the container might not have access to the necessary storage, leading to ContainerCreating state. But since the pod is scheduled, maybe the PVC is just not mounted yet, or there\'s an issue with the storage class.\n\nWait, the pod is scheduled, so the node is available. The issue might be with the image pull. Let me check the logs again. But earlier, trying to get logs resulted in an error because the container was still in ContainerCreating state. Maybe the image pull is taking longer than expected, or there\'s an error in pulling the image. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the image tag is incorrect. The image is "mongo:8.0.14-rc0", which is a release candidate. Perhaps the registry doesn\'t have that tag, or it\'s not accessible. I should check if the image exists. But how? Maybe using a tool like docker pull from the node, but since I can\'t do that, maybe check the events for any pull errors. \n\nLooking at the events again, there\'s only the "Pulling image" event. No error yet. So maybe the image is being pulled, but it\'s taking time. However, the pod has been in this state for 60 seconds, which might be too long. Alternatively, there could be a problem with the PVC. If the PVC isn\'t bound, the pod can\'t start. But the pod is scheduled, so the PVC might be bound, but the container can\'t access it. \n\nWait, the PVC is named "geo-pvc". Maybe checking the PVC status would help. Let me use the kubectl command to get the PVC details. If the PVC is in a pending state, that\'s a problem. So the next step is to check the status of the geo-pvc. \n\nSo the tool to use here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation". That will show if the PVC is bound or not. If it\'s pending, then the issue is with the storage provisioning. If it\'s bound, then maybe the container can\'t access the volume for some reason. \n\nAlternatively, if the image pull is failing, maybe the image name is wrong. But without more info, checking the PVC first makes sense. Let\'s proceed with that.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The pod is in "ContainerCreating" state, and the container image "mongo:8.0.14-rc0" is being pulled. To confirm if the image pull is successful or if there\'s an error, I need to check the PVC status for "geo-pvc" (as indicated in the pod\'s volume). If the PVC is not bound or has errors, it could prevent the container from starting. Running "kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation" will reveal the PVC\'s status and binding details.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 773, 'prompt_tokens': 5448, 'total_tokens': 6221, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-f4b3-7db0-ba74-14bd7e46c3db-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5448, 'output_tokens': 773, 'total_tokens': 6221}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9051981e-1d4b-4539-bebd-8c10fafdf1c6'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the MongoDB pod for the geo service isn't starting. So far, they've checked the deployments and pods, and found that several MongoDB pods are in ContainerCreating state. They tried to get logs but couldn't because the container isn't started yet. Then they described the pod and saw that the container is waiting to start. The pod's description shows that it's using a PersistentVolumeClaim called geo-pvc. \n\nThe next step is to check the status of that PVC. If the PVC isn't bound or has an error, that could be why the container can't start. The user needs to run kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation to see the PVC's status. If the PVC is not bound, there might be an issue with the PersistentVolume or the storage class. If there's an error, it could be a permissions issue or a problem with the volume configuration.\n\nSo the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command to get the PVC details. That will help determine if the storage is properly set up, which is crucial for the MongoDB pod to start. If the PVC is bound, then maybe the issue is elsewhere, like the container image or other configurations. If not, then the storage setup is the problem.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9837d82027f8f8eb', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 311, 'prompt_tokens': 6477, 'total_tokens': 6788, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cface-197c-7df3-89d2-a8e497234bbf-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exe