ble. The user might need to look into the pods associated with those deployments. For example, the mongodb-geo deployment has 0 pods available. So, using kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation would show the status of each pod. If the pods are crashing or not starting, that could indicate issues like configuration errors, resource limits, or image problems.\n\nAlternatively, checking the logs of the non-available pods could provide more insight. But first, confirming the pod statuses is essential. If the pods are in a CrashLoopBackOff state or have errors, that\'s a good starting point. Also, looking at the events related to those pods might show why they\'re not running. The user might need to check the pod details with kubectl describe pod <pod-name> to get more information on the specific issues. \n\nAnother angle is checking the resource quotas or limits in the namespace. If there\'s a LimitRange or ResourceQuota, it might be restricting the resources allocated to the pods, causing them to fail. But first, verifying the pod statuses and their logs would be more direct. So, the next tool call should be to get the list of pods in the namespace to see their current status.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The deployments for mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile, mongodb-rate, mongodb-recommendation, mongodb-reservation, mongodb-user, and rate are not available (0/1). To diagnose the root cause, I need to check the status of individual pods associated with these deployments. Running "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will reveal if pods are crashing, pending, or in error states, which will guide further investigation into specific failures.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 444, 'prompt_tokens': 3239, 'total_tokens': 3683, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-76b0-7303-ac62-954bf0ac0e33-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3239, 'output_tokens': 444, 'total_tokens': 3683}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0ffd4886-e8e1-4da9-9ac4-5b22586539c8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their hotel reservation application, which has multiple microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl to get services and deployments. The deployments for several MongoDB services and the \'rate\' service are showing 0/1, meaning they\'re not available. The next step is to check the pods associated with these deployments to see why they\'re not running.\n\nThe user chose to run "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to get the pod statuses. This makes sense because if the deployments aren\'t available, the pods might be in a crash loop, pending, or have errors. By checking the pods, they can get more detailed information about what\'s going wrong. \n\nOnce they have the pod statuses, they can look for patterns. For example, if pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, it might indicate application errors or configuration issues. If they\'re Pending, there could be resource constraints or scheduling problems. The output from this command will help pinpoint which specific pods are failing and why, leading to the root cause of the deployment issues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8d6bf51f7c0ef378', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 258, 'prompt_tokens': 4246, 'total_tokens': 4504, 'completion_tokens_details': None,