se why the MongoDB geo pod is in a ContainerCreating state. From the previous steps, we know that the pod is pending, and the container is waiting to start. The describe pod output shows that the container is pulling the image "mongo:8.0.14-rc0". \n\nFirst, I need to check if the image pull is successful. The event mentions "Pulling image", but it doesn\'t say if it\'s completed. If the image pull fails, the pod won\'t start. So maybe the image isn\'t available in the registry, or there\'s a network issue preventing it from being pulled.\n\nAnother thing to check is the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) mentioned in the volumes section. The geo PVC is being used, and if that\'s not properly provisioned, the container might not have access to the necessary storage, leading to ContainerCreating state. But since the pod is scheduled, maybe the PVC is just not mounted yet, or there\'s an issue with the storage class.\n\nWait, the pod is scheduled, so the node is available. The issue might be with the image pull. Let me check the logs again. But earlier, trying to get logs resulted in an error because the container was still in ContainerCreating state. Maybe the image pull is taking longer than expected, or there\'s an error in pulling the image. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the image tag is incorrect. The image is "mongo:8.0.14-rc0", which is a release candidate. Perhaps the registry doesn\'t have that tag, or it\'s not accessible. I should check if the image exists. But how? Maybe using a tool like docker pull from the node, but since I can\'t do that, maybe check the events for any pull errors. \n\nLooking at the events again, there\'s only the "Pulling image" event. No error yet. So maybe the image is being pulled, but it\'s taking time. However, the pod has been in this state for 60 seconds, which might be too long. Alternatively, there could be a problem with the PVC. If the PVC isn\'t bound, the pod can\'t start. But the pod is scheduled, so the PVC might be bound, but the container can\'t access it. \n\nWait, the PVC is named "geo-pvc". Maybe checking the PVC status would help. Let me use the kubectl command to get the PVC details. If the PVC is in a pending state, that\'s a problem. So the next step is to check the status of the geo-pvc. \n\nSo the tool to use here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation". That will show if the PVC is bound or not. If it\'s pending, then the issue is with the storage provisioning. If it\'s bound, then maybe the container can\'t access the volume for some reason. \n\nAlternatively, if the image pull is failing, maybe the image name is wrong. But without more info, checking the PVC first makes sense. Let\'s proceed with that.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The pod is in "ContainerCreating" state, and the container image "mongo:8.0.14-rc0" is being pulled. To confirm if the image pull is successful or if there\'s an error, I need to check the PVC status for "geo-pvc" (as indicated in the pod\'s volume). If the PVC is not bound or has errors, it could prevent the container from starting. Running "kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation" will reveal the PVC\'s status and binding details.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 773, 'prompt_tokens': 5448, 'total_tokens': 6221, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-f4b3-7db0-ba74-14bd7e46c3db-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5448, 'output_tokens': 773, 'total_tokens': 6221}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9051981e-1d4b-4539-bebd-8c10fafdf1c6'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the MongoDB pod for the geo service isn't starting. So far, they've checked the deployments and pods, and found that several MongoDB pods are in ContainerCreating state. They tried to get logs but couldn't because the container isn't started yet. Then they described the pod and saw that the container is waiting to start. The pod's description shows that it's using a PersistentVolumeClaim called geo-pvc. \n\nThe next step is to check the status of that PVC. If the PVC isn't bound or has an error, that could be why the container can't start. The user needs to run kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation to see the PVC's status. If the PVC is not bound, there might be an issue with the PersistentVolume or the storage class. If there's an error, it could be a permissions issue or a problem with the volume configuration.\n\nSo the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command to get the PVC details. That will help determine if the storage is properly set up, which is crucial for the MongoDB pod to start. If the PVC is bound, then maybe the issue is elsewhere, like the container image or other configurations. If not, then the storage setup is the problem.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9837d82027f8f8eb', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 311, 'prompt_tokens': 6477, 'total_tokens': 6788, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cface-197c-7df3-89d2-a8e497234bbf-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pvc geo-pvc -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9837d82027f8f8eb', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6477, 'output_tokens': 311, 'total_tokens': 6788}), ToolMessage(content='NAME      STATUS   VOLUME                                     CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS       VOLUMEATTRIBUTESCLASS   AGE\ngeo-pvc   Bound    pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652   1Gi        RWO            openebs-hostpath   <unset>                 3m40s\n', id='f306f0c1-9ae0-408b-8c95-67d90f168523', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9837d82027f8f8eb'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f421174e-a713-4ade-afaa-42c3ed99f2b3'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the MongoDB pod for the geo service isn\'t starting. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments, pods, and found that the pod is in ContainerCreating state. The logs weren\'t available yet, so they used kubectl describe pod, which showed that the container is waiting to start. The PVC for the volume is Bound, so that\'s not the issue. \n\nNow, the next step is to check if the PVC is properly attached and if there are any issues with the volume. Since the PVC is Bound, maybe the problem is with the volume itself. They should check the PersistentVolume (PV) associated with the PVC. If the PV is not available or has errors, that could prevent the container from starting. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the details of the PV linked to the geo-pvc. Using kubectl get pv would show the status of the PV. If the PV is in an error state or not bound, that would explain why the container can\'t start. Also, checking the events related to the PV might give more clues. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is with the container image. The image being pulled is mongo:8.0.14-rc0, which is a release candidate. Maybe there\'s an issue with that specific image version. But since the user already checked the image pull, and the event shows Pulling image, maybe it\'s still in progress. Wait, the event says "Pulling image" but the container is still in ContainerCreating. Maybe the image pull is failing, but the user hasn\'t checked the logs for the image pull. \n\nWait, the user tried to get logs for the container, but it was in ContainerCreating state. Maybe they should check the events for the pod again to see if there\'s an error related to the image pull. Or check the node\'s logs for the kubelet to see if there\'s an error pulling the image. But the available tools are limited to the ones provided. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the container can\'t start because the volume isn\'t mounted properly. The PVC is Bound, but the PV might not be correctly configured. So checking the PV details would be the next step. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to get the PV associated with geo-pvc. The command would be kubectl get pv pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652. Wait, but the user can\'t know the PV name directly. However, the PVC\'s status is Bound, so the PV name is listed in the PVC details. But in the previous response, the PVC\'s VOLUME column is pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652. Wait, no, the PVC\'s VOLUME column shows the PV name. Wait, in the output of kubectl get pvc, the VOLUME column is the PV name. So the PV name is pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652? Wait, no, the PVC\'s VOLUME column is the PV name. Wait, in the output:\n\nNAME      STATUS   VOLUME                                     CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS       VOLUMEATTRIBUTESCLASS   AGE\ngeo-pvc   Bound    pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652   1Gi        RWO            openebs-hostpath   <unset>                 3m40s\n\nSo the VOLUME column is pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652. Wait, but that\'s the same as the PVC name. That doesn\'t make sense. Wait, maybe the PV name is different. Wait, perhaps the user made a mistake here. Wait, the PVC\'s VOLUME column should show the PV name. If the PVC is Bound, then the PV name is listed there. But in the output, the VOLUME column is pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652, which is the same as the PVC name. That\'s not possible. Wait, maybe the user is using a tool that\'s not showing the PV name correctly. Or maybe there\'s a typo. Alternatively, maybe the PV is named differently. \n\nWait, perhaps the user should check the PV details. So the next step is to get the PV details. The command would be kubectl get pv <pv-name>. But since the user doesn\'t know the PV name, they need to find it. However, in the PVC output, the VOLUME column is the PV name. So if the PVC is Bound, the PV name is listed there. But in the output, the VOLUME column is pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652, which is the same as the PVC name. That\'s not correct. Wait, maybe the user is using a tool that\'s not displaying the PV name correctly. Alternatively, maybe the PV is named the same as the PVC, which is possible. \n\nIn any case, the user should check the PV details. So the next tool call would be to get the PV details. But the user can\'t know the PV name unless they check the PVC\'s details. Wait, the PVC\'s VOLUME column is the PV name. So the PV name is pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652. So the command would be kubectl get pv pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652. \n\nBut the user can\'t do that unless they have the PV name. Alternatively, maybe the user should check the events for the PV. But the available tools are limited. So the next step is to get the PV details. \n\nSo the tool call would be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pv pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652 -n hotel-reservation". But the user needs to make sure that the PV name is correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the PV is not properly configured, leading to the container not being able to start. So checking the PV status is the next step.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The PVC `geo-pvc` is bound, but the container is still in "ContainerCreating" state. To confirm if the volume is properly attached and accessible, I need to check the status of the associated PersistentVolume (PV). If the PV is not available or has errors, it could prevent the container from starting. Running "kubectl get pv pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652 -n hotel-reservation" will reveal the PV\'s status and any potential issues with the storage configuration.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1609, 'prompt_tokens': 5757, 'total_tokens': 7366, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cface-2b61-74d3-8923-69eb50e5fe23-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5757, 'output_tokens': 1609, 'total_tokens': 7366}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='65f5e1eb-93b4-4054-b1af-2d9d78b3ff2b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the MongoDB pod for the geo service isn\'t starting. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments, pods, and found that the pod is in ContainerCreating state. The logs aren\'t available yet, so they used kubectl describe pod to get more details. The describe output showed that the pod is pending, and the container is waiting to start. The PVC geo-pvc is bound, but the PV might be the issue.\n\nThe next step is to check the PersistentVolume (PV) associated with the PVC. The PVC\'s name is pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652. By running kubectl get pv on that name, they can see if the PV is available and properly configured. If the PV is not available or has errors, that could be why the container isn\'t starting. So the tool call should be to get the PV details. That makes sense because if the PV is in an error state or not properly attached, the pod can\'t access the storage, leading to ContainerCreating state. Let\'s make sure the command is correct and the namespace is specified. The command would be "kubectl get pv pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652 -n hotel-reservation".\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pv pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652 -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8766de9585e0f478', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 375, 'prompt_tokens': 6801, 'total_tokens': 7176, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cface-77cc-7e51-b521-f7245258f6b7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pv pvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652 -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8766de9585e0f478', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6801, 'output_tokens': 375, 'total_tokens': 7176}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                       CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   RECLAIM POLICY   STATUS   CLAIM                       STORAGECLASS       VOLUMEATTRIBUTESCLASS   REASON   AGE\npvc-4e872310-af4b-42a8-ab9e-18fbd9d31652   1Gi        RWO            Delete           Bound    hotel-reservation/geo-pvc   openebs-hostpath   <unset>                          3m45s\n', id='a3601a38-406b-408b-b193-d85eaba0ce33', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8766de9585e0f478'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c01d0272-2859-4f02-8f17-767efdba459c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the MongoDB pod for the geo service isn\'t starting. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments, pods, PVCs, and PVs. The pod is in ContainerCreating state, and the PVC is bound to a PV. The image is being pulled, but maybe there\'s an issue with the container itself.\n\nHmm, maybe the container isn\'t starting because of a problem with the MongoDB configuration or the init scripts. The pod\'s describe output mentioned volumes like init-script and failure-script. Maybe the init scripts are causing an issue. The user should check the logs of the init scripts or see if there are any errors in the pod\'s events related to those scripts.\n\nWait, the user tried to get logs for the main container but got an error because it\'s still in ContainerCreating. Maybe they need to check the logs of the init scripts or the failure script. Alternatively, maybe the container image is incorrect or there\'s a version mismatch. The image used is mongo:8.0.14-rc0, which is a release candidate. Maybe that\'s not a stable version, or there\'s a missing dependency.\n\nAnother angle: the pod\'s environment variables or configuration files might be missing. The describe output shows no environment variables, but maybe the ConfigMaps (init-script and failure-script) are not properly configured. The user could check the ConfigMaps to see if the scripts are correct or if there\'s an error in them.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the container is waiting for a specific resource that\'s not available. The PVC is bound, but maybe the volume isn\'t mounted correctly. The user could check the pod\'s events for any messages related to volume mounting failures. Also, checking the node\'s status to ensure it\'s healthy and has enough resources might help. But since the PVC and PV are bound, the issue is more likely within the container or the scripts.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The PVC and PV are correctly bound, but the container is still in "ContainerCreating" state. This suggests the issue may lie with the container image, init scripts, or configuration. To investigate further, I need to check the logs of the init scripts or failure scripts mounted in the pod. Running "kubectl logs mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation --all-containers" will capture logs from all containers, including any errors during initialization or script execution.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 514, 'prompt_tokens': 6125, 'total_tokens': 6639, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cface-8c79-7993-ad08-4a6d0044d523-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6125, 'output_tokens': 514, 'total_tokens': 6639}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='55a15dd7-3f05-4a4a-bb68-c21cd60b585f'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the MongoDB pod for the geo service isn\'t starting. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments, pods, PVCs, and PVs, and everything seems to be bound and available. The pod is in a ContainerCreating state, and the image is being pulled, but there\'s no error yet.\n\nThe next step is to check the logs from all containers in the pod, including any init scripts or failure scripts that might be running. The user mentioned that the pod has volumes mounted from ConfigMaps named "mongo-geo-script" and "failure-admin-geo". These ConfigMaps might contain scripts that run during initialization or on failure, which could be causing the delay.\n\nBy using the command "kubectl logs mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation --all-containers", they can get logs from all containers, not just the main MongoDB container. This might reveal any errors from the init scripts or failure scripts that are being executed. If there\'s a problem with the scripts, like a missing file or a syntax error, it could prevent the MongoDB container from starting properly.\n\nAlternatively, if the scripts are trying to access the PVC or perform some setup tasks that are failing, the logs would show that. Since the PVC is bound and the PV is available, the issue is likely in the scripts or the container configuration. Checking these logs should provide more insight into why the container isn\'t transitioning to the Running state.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation --all-containers"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93e42b38ae6f8743', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 365, 'prompt_tokens': 7149, 'total_tokens': 7514, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cface-a517-75d3-8421-2e876b7b23b4-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation --all-containers'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-93e42b38ae6f8743', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7149, 'output_tokens': 365, 'total_tokens': 7514}), ToolMessage(content='{"t":{"$date":"2026-03-17T07:59:14.581+00:00"},"s":"I",  "c":"CONTROL",  "id