ons, and any errors during creation. This will help identify issues like image pull failures, missing volumes, or configuration errors.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 293, 'prompt_tokens': 4387, 'total_tokens': 4680, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfacd-d796-76b2-baa1-877dbeeb4329-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4387, 'output_tokens': 293, 'total_tokens': 4680}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7049a594-6206-4f3f-829f-e53d6e4fd3db'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the MongoDB pods are in ContainerCreating state. They tried to get logs but got an error because the container isn\'t started yet. So the next step is to use kubectl describe pod to get more details about the pod\'s status and events.\n\nThe command they need to run is "kubectl describe pod mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation". This will show the pod\'s events, which might indicate why it\'s stuck. Common issues could be problems with the container image, volume mounts, or resource limits. By checking the describe output, they can look for events like "Failed to create container" or "Image pull backoff" to pinpoint the cause. Once they have that info, they can proceed to fix the specific issue, maybe by checking the image name, storage configurations, or resource requests.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b02865cfbddc7fca', 'type': 'functio