R\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 455, 'prompt_tokens': 15763, 'total_tokens': 16218, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-68b4-7e90-8843-70a1a42b930a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 15763, 'output_tokens': 455, 'total_tokens': 16218}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a1614d28-c281-4812-a584-254a524f2559'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in a Kubernetes cluster that are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments, pods, and logs. The logs showed that the MongoDB instance starts up but then fails during the init script execution. The init script is using single quotes around commands, which prevents environment variables like $ADMIN_USER and $ADMIN_PWD from being expanded. That means the script is trying to use literal strings like $ADMIN_USER instead of the actual values "admin" and "admin".\n\nThe user tried to edit the ConfigMap directly using kubectl edit, but that\'s not allowed. So the next step is to modify the ConfigMap\'s data by retrieving it, updating the script, and reapplying it. The plan is to replace the single quotes with double quotes in the script\'s content so that the variables get expanded correctly. \n\nFirst, they need to get the current ConfigMap as YAML, modify the script content, and then apply the updated ConfigMap. The command they used in the tool call does that: it exports the ConfigMap to a file, uses sed to replace single quote