nd justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7cb70069-2718-4677-8c44-1f72215d1ff1'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where several MongoDB pods are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. They tried fixing the init script by changing single quotes to double quotes for variable expansion, but the pods are still not running. \n\nFirst, I need to check if the fix was applied correctly. The previous step used sed to replace single quotes with double quotes in the ConfigMap. Maybe the script wasn\'t updated properly, or there are other issues. Let me verify the ConfigMap content again. \n\nWait, the user tried to edit the ConfigMap with kubectl edit, but that\'s not allowed. Instead, they used a command to get the ConfigMap, modify it, and apply it. The sed command was supposed to replace single quotes with double quotes. But maybe the sed command didn\'t work as intended. For example, if the script uses single quotes around the variables, replacing them with double quotes might not be sufficient. Also, maybe there are other parts of the script that need adjustment.\n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB pods are failing for reasons other than the init script. Even after fixing the script, there could be authentication issues, incorrect database names, or permission problems. The init script creates admin and root users with specific roles, but if the database name is wrong or the roles aren\'t properly assigned, the users might not have the necessary permissions, leading to crashes.\n\nI should check the logs of the MongoDB pods again to see if there are any errors after the ConfigMap was updated. The previous logs didn\'t show errors, but maybe after the fix, there are new errors. Also, checking the events for the pods could provide more insight into why they\'re crashing. \n\nAdditionally, the user might need to check if the MongoDB images are correctly configured. The init script uses --auth, which enables authentication. If the users aren\'t created correctly, the MongoDB instance might fail to start. The script\'s commands to create users and grant roles might have syntax errors or be executed before the database is fully initialized.\n\nAnother angle: the init script might be running before MongoDB is fully ready. The script has a loop to wait for MongoDB to start, but maybe the delay is insufficient. Increasing the wait time or checking if the MongoDB instance is ready before proceeding could help. \n\nAlso, the ConfigMap\'s script might have other issues, like incorrect database names or roles. For example, the TARGET_DB is set to "geo-db", but if the actual database name is different, the grant commands would fail. \n\nTo summarize, the next steps should be:\n1. Verify the updated ConfigMap content to ensure variables are correctly using double quotes.\n2. Check the MongoDB pod logs again for any errors after the fix.\n3. Examine the events and status of the pods to identify any new issues.\n4. Ensure that the init script\'s commands are correctly formatted and that the database names and roles are accurate.\n5. If the problem persists, consider checking the MongoDB configuration and ensuring that the init script is properly mounted and executed.\n\nBy addressing these areas, the user can determine if the root cause was the variable interpolation issue or another factor causing the MongoDB pods to crash.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The MongoDB pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff, indicating the init script issue might not be fully resolved. I need to recheck the script\'s variable interpolation and ensure the ConfigMap was updated correctly. Re-inspecting the script\'s content will confirm if double quotes replaced single quotes properly.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 756, 'prompt_tokens': 16981, 'total_tokens': 17737, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-c9e9-7961-b218-6f039081620d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 16981, 'output_tokens': 756, 'total_tokens': 17737}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1eb6817c-fc59-494e-92f2-1498cd605406'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in CrashLoopBackOff, which means they're crashing repeatedly. Earlier steps showed that the init script was using single quotes, which prevented environment variables from expanding. The user tried to fix that by modifying the ConfigMap to use double quotes, but the pods are still crashing.\n\nFirst, I need to verify if the ConfigMap was actually updated correctly. The user ran a command to get the ConfigMap's YAML, modify the script, and apply it again. But maybe the sed command didn't work as intended. Let me check the tool response. The user's last command was to get the ConfigMap's YAML, but the response isn't shown. However, the pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff, so the fix might not have taken effect.\n\nPerhaps the sed command didn't replace all instances of single quotes with double quotes. The script might still have some single-quoted sections. Alternatively, the ConfigMap might not have been updated properly. Another possibility is that the MongoDB pods are failing for a different reason now, like authentication issues despite the script changes.\n\nI should check the logs of the MongoDB pod again to see if there are new error messages. If the script is still using single quotes, the commands like creating users would fail because the variables like $ADMIN_USER wouldn't be replaced. The logs might show errors related to authentication or user creation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is not running correctly. The pod might be starting the MongoDB container but failing during the init phase. The logs from the init container could provide more clues. If the script is still using single quotes, the mongo commands would execute without the environment variables, leading to authentication failures.\n\nAnother angle: the user tried to apply the ConfigMap changes, but maybe the deployment isn't rolling out correctly. The MongoDB deployment might be stuck in a state where it's not picking up the new ConfigMap. Checking the deployment's rollout status or events could help. Also, ensuring that the ConfigMap is correctly referenced in the deployment's init container.\n\nWait, the user's last tool call was to get the ConfigMap's YAML. The response wasn't provided, but if the script still has single quotes, the issue persists. So, the next step should be to re-examine the ConfigMap's data to confirm the changes. If the script is still using single quotes, the user needs to correct that again. Alternatively, check the pod logs for more detailed error messages that indicate why the init script is failing.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb2920a9910e4513', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 564, 'prompt_tokens': 18103, 'total_tokens': 18667, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-f17d-7e92-bd21-589d7754b7da-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb2920a9910e4513', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 18103, 'output_tokens': 564, 'total_tokens': 18667}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\ndata:\n  k8s-geo-mongo.sh: |-\n    #!/bin/bash\n\n\n    ADMIN_USER="admin"\n    ADMIN_PWD="admin"\n\n    ROOT_USER="root"\n    ROOT_PWD="root"\n\n    TARGET_DB="geo-db"\n    READ_WRITE_ROLE="readWrite"\n\n    echo "Waiting for MongoDB to start..."\n    until mongo --eval "print(\'waited for connection\')" > /dev/null 2>&1; do\n      sleep 1\n    done\n    echo "MongoDB started"\n\n    # Create the admin user (will fail if the user already exists)\n    echo "Creating admin user..."\n    mongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ADMIN_USER\', pwd: \'$ADMIN_PWD\', roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"\n\n    # Grant readWrite role on the target database\n    echo "Granting readWrite role to $ADMIN_USER on $TARGET_DB database..."\n    mongo admin -u $ADMIN_USER -p $ADMIN_PWD --authenticationDatabase admin \\\n         --eval "db.grantRolesToUser(\'$ADMIN_USER\', [{role: \'$READ_WRITE_ROLE\', db: \'$TARGET_DB\'}]);"\n\n    # Create the root user (will fail if the user already exists)\n    echo "Creating root user..."\n    mongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ROOT_USER\', pwd: \'$ROOT_PWD\', roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"\n\n    # Grant readWrite role on the target database to root\n    echo "Granting readWrite role to $ROOT_USER on $TARGET_DB database..."\n    mongo admin -u $ROOT_USER -p $ROOT_PWD --authenticationDatabase admin \\\n         --eval "db.grantRolesToUser(\'$ROOT_USER\', [{role: \'$READ_WRITE_ROLE\', db: \'$TARGET_DB\'}]);"\n\n\n    echo "Initialization script completed"\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-17T07:55:19Z"\n  name: mongo-geo-script\n  namespace: hotel-reservation\n  resourceVersion: "88569"\n  uid: 9dea9eef-f9bb-4bee-af2f-881de57eb57a\n', id='9ef0842c-5d2e-4d91-ac2b-0d6dd5145452', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bb2920a9910e4513'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c30bf388-037d-49d8-85d9-ac8ecfeaf0ce')]]
