 logs show the MongoDB service starts up, does recovery, and then seems to exit. The init script is supposed to create admin and root users, but the logs don\'t show any errors from that. The previous logs from the pod\'s previous run didn\'t show any errors either.\n\nWait, the user checked the init script\'s content and found that it uses "admin/admin" and "root/root" credentials. Maybe the issue is that the script is trying to create users before MongoDB is fully ready, or the credentials are incorrect. But the logs don\'t show any authentication errors. Hmm.\n\nAnother possibility is that the init script is failing because the MongoDB instance isn\'t ready when the script runs. The script has a loop to wait for MongoDB to start, but maybe that\'s not sufficient. Or maybe the script is trying to authenticate with the admin user before the user is created. Let me check the script again.\n\nLooking at the script, it first waits for MongoDB to start, then tries to create the admin user. If the admin user doesn\'t exist yet, the create command would fail. Wait, but the script is supposed to create the admin user. If the user already exists, it would fail. But if the script is run multiple times, maybe it\'s failing on subsequent runs because the user already exists. But the pod is crashing, so maybe the script is failing during the creation steps.\n\nWait, the logs from the current run don\'t show any errors from the script. The last lines are about MongoDB starting up and recovery. Maybe the script is not being executed at all. Or maybe the script is being run but not producing any output, so the logs don\'t show the error. Alternatively, the script might be failing silently due to permission issues or incorrect database configuration.\n\nAnother angle: the MongoDB pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, which means they crash and restart repeatedly. The init container might be failing, causing the pod to crash. The init container\'s script is part of the pod\'s lifecycle. If the init container fails, the pod can\'t proceed to the running state. But the logs from the pod\'s previous run didn\'t show any errors from the init script. Wait, the user tried to get the previous logs, but maybe the init script\'s output isn\'t captured in the logs. Or maybe the init script is not being run correctly.\n\nWait, the user checked the ConfigMap for the init script and found the script content. The script uses variables like $ADMIN_USER and $ADMIN_PWD, which are set to "admin" and "admin". Maybe the credentials are incorrect. For example, if the admin user already exists with a different password, the create command would fail. Or maybe the script is trying to connect to the wrong database or with incorrect authentication parameters.\n\nAlternatively, the script might be trying to authenticate with the admin user before the user is created. For instance, when creating the admin user, the script uses the admin database, but if the admin user doesn\'t exist yet, the command would fail. Wait, the script first creates the admin user, then grants roles. If the creation fails, the subsequent commands would also fail. But the logs don\'t show any errors from these steps. Maybe the script is not being executed because the init container is not running, or the script is not being sourced correctly.\n\nWait, the user checked the pod\'s logs, but maybe the init container\'s logs are not being captured. Or perhaps the init container is not part of the pod\'s lifecycle. Wait, the MongoDB pod\'s configuration includes a ConfigMap for the init script, which is mounted as a volume. The init script is part of the container\'s entry point or command. If the script is not being executed, that could be the issue.\n\nAlternatively, the script might be failing due to a syntax error. Let me check the script again. The script uses variables like $ADMIN_USER and $ADMIN_PWD, which are set in the script. The commands use these variables in the mongo commands. For example, the line "mongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ADMIN_USER\', pwd: \'$ADMIN_PWD\', roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"" uses the variables. If the variables are not properly interpolated, that could cause errors. But in the script, the variables are enclosed in single quotes, which would prevent variable expansion. Oh, wait! That\'s a problem. In bash, using single quotes around the command would prevent the variables from being expanded. So the script is actually using the literal strings "$ADMIN_USER" and "$ADMIN_PWD" instead of the actual values. That would cause the mongo commands to fail because the user and password are not set correctly. \n\nSo the root cause is that the script uses single quotes around the commands, which prevents variable substitution. The variables ADMIN_USER and ADMIN_PWD are set in the script, but they are not being used correctly. The correct approach would be to use double quotes or escape the variables so they are expanded. For example, changing the line to:\n\nmongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \\"$ADMIN_USER\\", pwd: \\"$ADMIN_PWD\\", roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"\n\nThis way, the variables are expanded to their actual values. If the script is using single quotes, the variables are not replaced, leading to incorrect commands and authentication failures, which would cause the init script to fail, leading to the pod crashing.\n\nSo the solution is to correct the script\'s syntax to use double quotes or escape the variables so that the actual values are used. Once that\'s fixed, the init script should create the users correctly, and the MongoDB pods should start without crashing.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The MongoDB init script (`k8s-geo-mongo.sh`) is failing due to incorrect variable interpolation. The script uses single quotes around commands, preventing environment variable expansion (e.g., `$ADMIN_USER` remains as literal text). This causes authentication failures when creating users. Fixing the script to use double quotes or escape variables will resolve the issue.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl edit configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1303, 'prompt_tokens': 15540, 'total_tokens': 16843, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-0dec-7bf3-8f30-761566d18ac2-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 15540, 'output_tokens': 1303, 'total_tokens': 16843}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='80c7ecce-e37c-4f32-8fd3-47a3706f66c9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in their hotel reservation application that are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments, pods, and logs. The logs show that MongoDB starts up but the init script is failing. The init script is using single quotes around commands, which means variables like $ADMIN_USER and $ROOT_USER aren\'t being expanded. That\'s probably why the authentication is failing—because the script is using "admin" and "root" as literals instead of the actual variables.\n\nSo, the root cause is the incorrect use of single quotes in the init script. The fix should be to change those single quotes to double quotes so that the variables get expanded properly. But since the user can\'t edit the ConfigMap directly through the CLI without using kubectl edit, they need to use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to edit the ConfigMap. \n\nWait, the user tried to describe the ConfigMap earlier and saw the script content. Now they need to edit it. The tool call should be to kubectl edit the configmap mongo-geo-script in the hotel-reservation namespace. That way, they can modify the script to use double quotes or escape the variables. Once that\'s done, the script should correctly use the admin and root credentials, allowing the MongoDB container to start properly. After editing, they should check if the pods are now running without issues. If not, maybe there\'s another problem, but this seems like the main issue here.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl edit configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-99156de1d3fb3f08', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 358, 'prompt_tokens': 16672, 'total_tokens': 17030, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-52b2-70f2-bafc-2a038f9be5ef-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl edit configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-99156de1d3fb3f08', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 16672, 'output_tokens': 358, 'total_tokens': 17030}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected (ValueError): Interactive command kubectl edit detected. Such commands are not supported.', id='9a6d1845-fb43-4ad0-b46a-75c750657323', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-99156de1d3fb3f08'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='73c6a2a5-a311-4559-b155-8134c71112cf'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the MongoDB init script issue. The problem is that the script uses single quotes, which prevent variable expansion. So the credentials like $ADMIN_USER aren\'t being replaced with the actual values, leading to authentication failures.\n\nEarlier, they tried to edit the ConfigMap using `kubectl edit`, but that\'s an interactive command and isn\'t allowed here. So they need another way to update the script. The solution is to modify the ConfigMap\'s data directly. \n\nThe correct approach would be to use `kubectl get configmap` to retrieve the current content, edit the script in a text editor, and then apply the changes. But since the tool doesn\'t support interactive edits, they can use `kubectl apply` with a modified YAML file. \n\nFirst, they should get the existing ConfigMap. Then, update the script content to use double quotes or escape the variables so that the environment variables are properly expanded. After making the changes, they can create a new ConfigMap with the updated content. \n\nWait, but the user can\'t use `kubectl edit` directly. So they need to create a new ConfigMap YAML file, update the script content, and apply it. That way, they can replace the old ConfigMap with the corrected one. \n\nSo the next step is to get the current ConfigMap data, modify the script\'s content to fix the variable interpolation, and then apply the changes using `kubectl apply -f`. This method avoids interactive commands and adheres to the tool\'s constraints.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: Since `kubectl edit` is not supported, I need to directly update the ConfigMap\'s data by retrieving it, modifying the script content to use double quotes for variable expansion, and reapplying the ConfigMap. This ensures environment variables like `$ADMIN_USER` are properly interpolated.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml > mongo-geo-script.yaml && sed -i \'s/\\\'$ADMIN_USER\\\'/\\"$ADMIN_USER\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 455, 'prompt_tokens': 15763, 'total_tokens': 16218, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-68b4-7e90-8843-70a1a42b930a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 15763, 'output_tokens': 455, 'total_tokens': 16218}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a1614d28-c281-4812-a584-254a524f2559'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in a Kubernetes cluster that are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments, pods, and logs. The logs showed that the MongoDB instance starts up but then fails during the init script execution. The init script is using single quotes around commands, which prevents environment variables like $ADMIN_USER and $ADMIN_PWD from being expanded. That means the script is trying to use literal strings like $ADMIN_USER instead of the actual values "admin" and "admin".\n\nThe user tried to edit the ConfigMap directly using kubectl edit, but that\'s not allowed. So the next step is to modify the ConfigMap\'s data by retrieving it, updating the script, and reapplying it. The plan is to replace the single quotes with double quotes in the script\'s content so that the variables get expanded correctly. \n\nFirst, they need to get the current ConfigMap as YAML, modify the script content, and then apply the updated ConfigMap. The command they used in the tool call does that: it exports the ConfigMap to a file, uses sed to replace single quotes with double quotes around the variables, and then applies the changes. \n\nAfter applying the updated ConfigMap, the init script should now correctly use the environment variables, creating the admin and root users with the right credentials. This should resolve the authentication issues causing the MongoDB pods to fail. Once the ConfigMap is updated, they should monitor the pods to see if they start successfully. If the pods remain in CrashLoopBackOff, they might need to check other factors like resource limits or network issues, but the primary issue here seems to be the script\'s variable interpolation.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml > mongo-geo-script.yaml && sed -i \'s/\\\'$ADMIN_USER\\\'/\\"$ADMIN_USER\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 443, 'prompt_tokens': 16926, 'total_tokens': 17369, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-8044-76b1-8904-ddca49bf0610-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 16926, 'output_tokens': 443, 'total_tokens': 17369}), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='78560546-3c71-444d-9384-f035dbe2f5a6'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in a Kubernetes cluster that are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments, pods, and ConfigMaps. The key issue seems to be with the init script for MongoDB, specifically the `k8s-geo-mongo.sh` script. \n\nThe logs show that the MongoDB instance starts up successfully, but the init script is failing. The script uses single quotes around commands, which means environment variables like `$ADMIN_USER` and `$ADMIN_PWD` aren\'t being expanded. Instead, they\'re treated as literal strings. For example, the script tries to create a user with the username "admin" and password "admin", but if the script isn\'t using double quotes, the variables aren\'t replaced, leading to authentication failures.\n\nThe user tried to edit the ConfigMap but couldn\'t because `kubectl edit` isn\'t supported. So they used a command to get the ConfigMap, modify the script to use double quotes, and reapply it. Now, after applying the changes, the next step is to check if the MongoDB pods are recovering. \n\nThey should verify if the pods are no longer in CrashLoopBackOff and if the init script is running correctly. If the pods start successfully, the issue is resolved. If not, they might need to check the logs again or ensure that the credentials are correct. But based on the previous steps, modifying the script to use double quotes should fix the variable expansion problem, allowing the script to create the users properly. \n\nSo the next logical step is to check the status of the MongoDB pods again to confirm if the issue is resolved. If the pods are now running, then the mitigation is successful. If there\'s still an issue, they might need to look into other factors like resource limits or network issues, but the primary problem here seems to be the script\'s variable handling.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: After fixing the ConfigMap script to use double quotes for variable expansion, I need to verify if the MongoDB pods are now running successfully. This will confirm if the script issue was the root cause.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 474, 'prompt_tokens': 16035, 'total_tokens': 16509, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-9775-7b90-8671-91a8e48dbb6f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 16035, 'output_tokens': 474, 'total_tokens': 16509}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='90546c40-9701-4b5d-905e-e1cd67a39925'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where several MongoDB pods are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. They've been using kubectl commands to check the deployments, pods, and ConfigMaps. \n\nFirst, they checked the deployments and saw that the MongoDB pods have 0/1 available replicas. Then, they looked at the pod details and found that the containers are in CrashLoopBackOff. The logs showed MongoDB starting up but no errors, which was confusing. \n\nThey then checked the ConfigMap for the init script and found that the script uses single quotes, which prevents variable expansion. The variables like $ADMIN_USER and $ROOT_USER weren't being replaced, leading to authentication failures. \n\nThe user tried to edit the ConfigMap but couldn't because kubectl edit isn't supported. So, they decided to retrieve the ConfigMap, modify the script to use double quotes, and reapply it. \n\nNow, after applying the fix, they need to check if the pods are running. The last command they ran was kubectl get pods, which shows the MongoDB pods are still not running. But maybe the fix hasn't taken effect yet. They might need to wait a bit for the pods to restart and then check again. \n\nAlternatively, they could check the pod events or logs again to see if there are any new errors. But since the user is in the mitigation phase, the next step is to confirm if the issue is resolved. If the pods are now in a Running state, then the problem is fixed. If not, they might need to look into other issues like resource limits or network problems. \n\nBut according to the previous steps, the script was fixed, so the pods should start correctly now. The user should wait a moment and then check the pod status again. If the pods are still not running, they might need to look into other factors, but based on the information, the main issue was the script's variable handling.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-90683908a4e8a4e9', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 446, 'prompt_tokens': 17130, 'total_tokens': 17576, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-b010-7ea2-83da-df1b871194bd-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-90683908a4e8a4e9', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 17130, 'output_tokens': 446, 'total_tokens': 17576}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                     READY   STATUS             RESTARTS        AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-6s4xr                  1/1     Running            0               8m44s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-qcb4d                1/1     Running            0               8m44s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-s9b66                     1/1     Running            2 (7m51s ago)   8m43s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-hgczs                  1/1     Running            0               8m40s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-hgpdc        1/1     Running            0               8m37s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-qlwq9           1/1     Running            0               8m32s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-8644q       1/1     Running            0               8m21s\nmongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl             0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (62s ago)     6m18s\nmongodb-profile-76fb8d4b68-nl5tj         0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (67s ago)     6m16s\nmongodb-rate-b9589b8d-n67gv              0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (46s ago)     6m11s\nmongodb-recommendation-cf88b8cc4-4zglc   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (23s ago)     6m7s\nmongodb-reservation-58fdc8958-h8rbb      0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (41s ago)     6m8s\nmongodb-user-799846599d-nf6z6            0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (32s ago)     6m6s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-8vctx                 1/1     Running            2 (7m35s ago)   8m35s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-h6rfh                    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (2m44s ago)   8m29s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-hd8gm          1/1     Running            2 (7m10s ago)   8m24s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-6fqt5             0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (2m39s ago)   8m16s\nsearch-5f668579c9-lgwr6                  1/1     Running            0               8m12s\nuser-6548d868cd-md9hd                    1/1     Running            2 (6m52s ago)   8m7s\nwrk2-job-dnjvw                           1/1     Running            0               6m26s\n', id='90a5b444-fbfa-40b7-86e3-47b01acf34d0', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-90683908a4e8a4e9'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7cb70069-2718-4677-8c44-1f72215d1ff1'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where several MongoDB pods are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. They tried fixing the init script by changing single quotes to double quotes for variable expansion, but the pods are still not running. \n\nFirst, I need to check if the fix was applied correctly. The previous step used sed to replace single quotes with double quotes in the ConfigMap. Maybe the script wasn\'t updated properly, or there are other issues. Let me verify the ConfigMap content again. \n\nWait, the user tried to edit the ConfigMap with kubectl edit, but that\'s not allowed. Instead, they used a command to get the ConfigMap, modify it, and apply it. The sed command was supposed to replace single quotes with double quotes. But maybe the sed command didn\'t work as intended. For example, if the script uses single quotes around the variables, replacing them with double quotes might not be sufficient. Also, maybe there are other parts of the script that need adjustment.\n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB pods are failing for reasons other than the init script. Even after fixing the script, there could be authentication issues, incorrect database names, or permission problems. The init script creates admin and root users with specific roles, but if the database name is wrong or the roles aren\'t properly assigned, the users might not have the necessary permissions, leading to crashes.\n\nI should check the logs of the MongoDB pods again to see if there are any errors after the ConfigMap was updated. The previous logs didn\'t show errors, but maybe after the fix, there are new errors. Also, checking the events for the pods could provide more insight into why they\'re crashing. \n\nAdditionally, the user might need to check if the MongoDB images are correctly configured. The init script uses --auth, which enables authentication. If the users aren\'t created correctly, the MongoDB instance might fail to start. The script\'s commands to create users and grant roles might have syntax errors or be executed before the database is fully initialized.\n\nAnother angle: the init script might be running before MongoDB is fully ready. The script has a loop to wait for MongoDB to start, but maybe the delay is insufficient. Increasing the wait time or checking if the MongoDB instance is ready before proceeding could help. \n\nAlso, the ConfigMap\'s script might have other issues, like incorrect database names or roles. For example, the TARGET_DB is set to "geo-db", but if the actual database name is different, the grant commands would fail. \n\nTo summarize, the next steps should be:\n1. Verify the updated ConfigMap content to ensure variables are correctly using double quotes.\n2. Check the MongoDB pod logs again for any errors after the fix.\n3. Examine the events and status of the pods to identify any new issues.\n4. Ensure that the init script\'s commands are correctly formatted and that the database names and roles are accurate.\n5. If the problem persists, consider checking the MongoDB configuration and ensuring that the init script is properly mounted and executed.\n\nBy addressing these areas, the user can determine if the root cause was the variable interpolation issue or another factor causing the MongoDB pods to crash.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The MongoDB pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff, indicating the init script issue might not be fully resolved. I need to recheck the script\'s variable interpolation and ensure the ConfigMap was updated correctly. Re-inspecting the script\'s content will confirm if double quotes replaced single quotes properly.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 756, 'prompt_tokens': 16981, 'total_tokens': 17737, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-c9e9-7961-b218-6f039081620d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 16981, 'output_tokens': 756, 'total_tokens': 17737}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1eb6817c-fc59-494e-92f2-1498cd605406'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in CrashLoopBackOff, which means they're crashing repeatedly. Earlier steps showed that the init script was using single quotes, which prevented environment variables from expanding. The user tried to fix that by modifying the ConfigMap to use double quotes, but the pods are still crashing.\n\nFirst, I need to verify if the ConfigMap was actually updated correctly. The user ran a command to get the ConfigMap's YAML, modify the script, and apply it again. But maybe the sed command didn't work as intended. Let me check the tool response. The user's last command was to get the ConfigMap's YAML, but the response isn't shown. However, the pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff, so the fix might not have taken effect.\n\nPerhaps the sed command didn't replace all instances of single quotes with double quotes. The script might still have some single-quoted sections. Alternatively, the ConfigMap might not have been updated properly. Another possibility is that the MongoDB pods are failing for a different reason now, like authentication issues despite the script changes.\n\nI should check the logs of the MongoDB pod again to see if there are new error messages. If the script is still using single quotes, the commands like creating users would fail because the variables like $ADMIN_USER wouldn't be replaced. The logs might show errors related to authentication or user creation.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container is not running correctly. The pod might be starting the MongoDB container but failing during the init phase. The logs from the init container could provide more clues. If the script is still using single quotes, the mongo commands would execute without the environment variables, leading to authentication failures.\n\nAnother angle: the user tried to apply the ConfigMap changes, but maybe the deployment isn't rolling out correctly. The MongoDB deployment might be stuck in a state where it's not picking up the new ConfigMap. Checking the deployment's rollout status or events could help. Also, ensuring that the ConfigMap is correctly referenced in the deployment's init container.\n\nWait, the user's last tool call was to get the ConfigMap's YAML. The response wasn't provided, but if the script still has single quotes, the issue persists. So, the next step should be to re-examine the ConfigMap's data to confirm the changes. If the script is still using single quotes, the user needs to correct that again. Alternatively, check the pod logs for more detailed error messages that indicate why the init script is failing.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb2920a9910e4513', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 564, 'prompt_tokens': 18103, 'total_tokens': 18667, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad2-f17d-7e92-bd21-589d7754b7da-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bb2920a9910e4513', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 18103, 'output_tokens': 564, 'total_tokens': 18667}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\ndata:\n  k8s-geo-mongo.sh: |-\n    #!/bin/bash\n\n\n    ADMIN_USER="admin"\n    ADMIN_PWD="admin"\n\n    ROOT_USER="root"\n    ROOT_PWD="root"\n\n    TARGET_DB="geo-db"\n    READ_WRITE_ROLE="readWrite"\n\n    echo "Waiting for MongoDB to start..."\n    until mongo --eval "print(\'waited for connection\')" > /dev/null 2>&1; do\n      sleep 1\n    done\n    echo "MongoDB started"\n\n    # Create the admin user (will fail if the user already exists)\n    echo "Creating admin user..."\n    mongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ADMIN_USER\', pwd: \'$ADMIN_PWD\', roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"\n\n    # Grant readWrite role on the target database\n    echo "Granting readWrite role to $ADMIN_USER on $TARGET_DB database..."\n    mongo admin -u $ADMIN_USER -p $ADMIN_PWD --authenticationDatabase admin \\\n         --eval "db.grantRolesToUser(\'$ADMIN_USER\', [{role: \'$READ_WRITE_ROLE\', db: \'$TARGET_DB\'}]);"\n\n    # Create the root user (will fail if the user already exists)\n    echo "Creating root user..."\n    mongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ROOT_USER\', pwd: \'$ROOT_PWD\', roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"\n\n    # Grant readWrite role on the target database to root\n    echo "Granting readWrite role to $ROOT_USER on $TARGET_DB database..."\n    mongo admin -u $ROOT_USER -p $ROOT_PWD --authenticationDatabase admin \\\n         --eval "db.grantRolesToUser(\'$ROOT_USER\', [{role: \'$READ_WRITE_ROLE\', db: \'$TARGET_DB\'}]);"\n\n\n    echo "Initialization script completed"\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-17T07:55:19Z"\n  name: mongo-geo-script\n  namespace: hotel-reservation\n  resourceVersion: "88569"\n  uid: 9dea9eef-f9bb-4bee-af2f-881de57eb57a\n', id='9ef0842c-5d2e-4d91-ac2b-0d6dd5145452', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bb2920a9910e4513'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c30bf388-037d-49d8-85d9-ac8ecfeaf0ce'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in CrashLoopBackOff, and they\'ve already tried fixing the init script by changing single quotes to double quotes. But the pods are still crashing. \n\nLooking at the ConfigMap data, the script uses variables like $ADMIN_USER and $ADMIN_PWD. Wait, in the script, the variables are set with double quotes, but when they\'re used in the mongo commands, they\'re inside single quotes. For example, in the line mongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ADMIN_USER\', pwd: \'$ADMIN_PWD\', ...})", the variables are enclosed in single quotes. That means the shell isn\'t expanding them; they\'re treated as literal strings. So even though the ConfigMap was updated to use double quotes around the variable assignments, the actual usage in the script is still with single quotes, which prevents the variables from being substituted.\n\nSo the root cause is that the script uses single quotes around the variables in the mongo commands, which stops the shell from expanding the environment variables. The fix should be to change those single quotes to double quotes or use command substitution properly. \n\nThe user needs to modify the script in the ConfigMap to use double quotes around the variables in the mongo commands. For example, changing \'user: \'$ADMIN_USER\'\' to \'user: "$ADMIN_USER"\'. That way, the shell will replace $ADMIN_USER with its value before passing the command to mongo. \n\nSince the previous attempt to edit the ConfigMap didn\'t work because kubectl edit isn\'t supported, the next step is to retrieve the ConfigMap, modify the script content to use double quotes for variable expansion, and reapply it. Then, check if the pods start correctly after this change.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The script still uses single quotes around variables in mongo commands (e.g., `$ADMIN_USER` remains unexpanded). I need to fix the ConfigMap by replacing single quotes with double quotes for variable interpolation.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml > mongo-geo-script.yaml && sed -i \'s/\\\'$ADMIN_USER\\\'/\\"$ADMIN_USER\\"/g;s/\\\'$ADMIN_PWD\\\'/\\"$ADMIN_PWD\\"/g;s/\\\'$ROOT_USER\\\'/\\"$ROOT_USER\\"/g;s/\\\'$ROOT_PWD\\\'/\\"$ROOT_PWD\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=541, prompt_tokens=17669, total_tokens=18210, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad3-123c-7751-97c1-b96faa742f7b-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 17669, 'output_tokens': 541, 'total_tokens': 18210}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='95f607e2-601c-44a0-858e-8de7d0f56485')]]
