2026-03-17 08:05:09 - DEBUG - all.stratus.mitigation - [Loop 11] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='02d324ee-e2db-444a-b8ee-a9accc99ff74'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nHotel Reservation\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nhotel-reservation\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application that has several microservices, and they\'re trying to diagnose why some MongoDB pods are stuck in "ContainerCreating" state. \n\nFirst, they started by listing all the services and deployments in the namespace. Then, they checked the deployments and found that several MongoDB services like mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile, etc., have 0/1 replicas available. The next step was to check the pods, which showed that these MongoDB pods are in ContainerCreating state. \n\nThey then checked the PVCs and PVs, which were bound, so storage wasn\'t the issue. The next step was to look into the init scripts because the logs showed MongoDB starting up but the pod wasn\'t transitioning to Running. The init script, k8s-geo-mongo.sh, was supposed to create admin and root users, but the container was still stuck. \n\nLooking at the script, it uses credentials "admin/admin" and "root/root". The problem might be that these credentials are incorrect or the MongoDB instance isn\'t ready when the script runs. The logs didn\'t show errors, which is confusing. Maybe the script is failing silently or the MongoDB service isn\'t fully up when the script executes. \n\nThe user tried checking the logs again, but the output only showed the startup and recovery logs. The events for the pod didn\'t show any errors related to the init script. The root cause is likely the init script failing due to authentication issues with the incorrect credentials or the script running before MongoDB is ready. \n\nSo, the final answer is that the MongoDB geo service\'s container is failing to initialize because of authentication errors in the init script, possibly due to incorrect credentials or the script running too early. This prevents the container from moving to the\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='641c3772-ba47-4394-bd75-aab8e0cb1e22'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7848b7d6-db93-4f5c-b5fd-d02ea42c411b'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by understanding the problem. The user mentioned that some MongoDB pods are stuck in "ContainerCreating" state. The first step is to check the current state of the pods and deployments to identify which services are affected. \n\nI need to use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to get the list of deployments in the hotel-reservation namespace. This will help me see if any deployments are not functioning correctly. The command would be "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation". \n\nBy checking the deployments, I can determine if there are any replicas that are not available, which might indicate issues with the pods. If the deployments show that some MongoDB services have 0/1 replicas, that\'s a red flag. Then, I can proceed to check the specific pods related to those deployments to see their status and any associated errors.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: To identify which microservices are affected, I need to first check the current state of deployments and pods in the hotel-reservation namespace. This will help determine if any services are not running as expected and provide initial clues about potential faults.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 254, 'prompt_tokens': 2055, 'total_tokens': 2309, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad0-9c47-7b21-9475-4fabeb548a5a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2055, 'output_tokens': 254, 'total_tokens': 2309}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d511c7ee-96ac-43c8-a8cd-173969c8d2c2'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me start by understanding the user\'s problem. They mentioned a hotel reservation application with MongoDB pods stuck in "ContainerCreating" state. The user has already run some kubectl commands to check deployments and pods, but the issue persists.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the MongoDB pods aren\'t starting. The user checked the deployments and found that the replicas are at 0/1, and the pods are in ContainerCreating. They also checked PVCs and PVs, which were bound, so storage isn\'t the issue. The next step was looking into the init scripts, which might be failing due to incorrect credentials or timing issues.\n\nThe user tried checking the logs again but didn\'t see errors. The events for the pod didn\'t show any issues related to the init script. The root cause seems to be the init script using incorrect credentials ("admin/admin" and "root/root") or running before MongoDB is ready. \n\nTo confirm, I should check the init script\'s content. The user mentioned the script is k8s-geo-mongo.sh, which creates admin and root users. If the credentials are wrong, MongoDB might fail to start. Alternatively, the script might be executing too early, before the MongoDB service is fully up, leading to a hang.\n\nThe next step is to check the script\'s content. Using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool, I can describe the pod to get the init container\'s details. Wait, but the user already checked the logs. Maybe checking the script\'s content or the environment variables passed to it would help. Alternatively, checking the MongoDB logs for authentication errors could provide more insight.\n\nBut since the user can\'t access the file directly, maybe checking the environment variables or secrets used by the MongoDB pod would reveal the correct credentials. If the script is using hardcoded credentials, those might be incorrect. Alternatively, the script might be failing silently, so the logs aren\'t showing errors. \n\nAnother angle: the pod is in ContainerCreating, which usually means the container is starting but hasn\'t exited yet. If the init script is stuck, the container might be waiting for something. Maybe the MongoDB service isn\'t ready when the script runs, causing it to hang. \n\nTo verify, the user could check the pod\'s events for any errors related to the init container. They might also need to check the MongoDB logs for any authentication failures. If the credentials are wrong, MongoDB would log that. \n\nIn the previous steps, the user tried checking the logs but didn\'t see errors. Maybe the logs are not detailed enough, or the issue is with the init script\'s execution order. The root cause is likely the init script failing due to incorrect credentials or timing, preventing the container from transitioning to Running.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a36aab0972e8ee18', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 592, 'prompt_tokens': 3157, 'total_tokens': 3749, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad0-a83a-7562-80dc-336effbde49f-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a36aab0972e8ee18', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3157, 'output_tokens': 592, 'total_tokens': 3749}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                     READY   STATUS             RESTARTS        AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-6s4xr                  1/1     Running            0               6m32s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-qcb4d                1/1     Running            0               6m32s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-s9b66                     1/1     Running            2 (5m39s ago)   6m31s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-hgczs                  1/1     Running            0               6m28s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-hgpdc        1/1     Running            0               6m25s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-qlwq9           1/1     Running            0               6m20s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-8644q       1/1     Running            0               6m9s\nmongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl             0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4 (21s ago)     4m6s\nmongodb-profile-76fb8d4b68-nl5tj         0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4 (29s ago)     4m4s\nmongodb-rate-b9589b8d-n67gv              0/1     Error              4 (62s ago)     3m59s\nmongodb-recommendation-cf88b8cc4-4zglc   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   3 (34s ago)     3m55s\nmongodb-reservation-58fdc8958-h8rbb      0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   3 (58s ago)     3m56s\nmongodb-user-799846599d-nf6z6            0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   3 (41s ago)     3m54s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-8vctx                 1/1     Running            2 (5m23s ago)   6m23s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-h6rfh                    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (32s ago)     6m17s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-hd8gm          1/1     Running            2 (4m58s ago)   6m12s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-6fqt5             0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (27s ago)     6m4s\nsearch-5f668579c9-lgwr6                  1/1     Running            0               6m\nuser-6548d868cd-md9hd                    1/1     Running            2 (4m40s ago)   5m55s\nwrk2-job-dnjvw                           1/1     Running            0               4m14s\n', id='fc145dcd-8815-4e9b-9c28-e00bf9f2bdfb', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a36aab0972e8ee18'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ac202684-9195-4575-ba34-a89b46af4649'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where several MongoDB pods are in CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. The initial steps were to check the deployments and pods, which showed that the MongoDB-related pods are not running properly. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out why these MongoDB pods are failing. The next logical step is to check the specific deployments for these MongoDB services. The user mentioned services like mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile, etc. So, using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to get the details of these deployments would help. \n\nBy checking the deployment configurations, I can see if there are any issues with the replicas, image versions, or resource limits. For example, if the replicas are set to 0, that would explain why the pods aren\'t running. Alternatively, there might be an error in the container image or insufficient resources allocated.\n\nAnother possibility is that the pods are failing to start due to configuration errors. Checking the deployment\'s YAML might reveal if the containers are set up correctly, including environment variables, volumes, or init containers. Since the user mentioned that the init script is trying to create admin and root users, maybe there\'s an issue with the credentials or the script itself.\n\nAlso, looking at the events associated with the pods could provide more insight. The user should check the pod events to see if there are any errors related to the container startup, such as failed health checks, image pull errors, or issues with the init containers. \n\nSo, the next step is to get the details of the problematic deployments. Using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool with the command "kubectl describe deployments" for the affected MongoDB deployments would provide detailed information about their current state, events, and any errors that might be occurring. This will help pinpoint the root cause, whether it\'s a configuration issue, resource problem, or something else related to the container setup.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The MongoDB pods (e.g., mongodb-geo, mongodb-profile) are in CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. To diagnose the root cause, I need to inspect their deployment configurations, check for resource limits, image issues, or init container failures. Describing the deployments will reveal events, replica statuses, and potential misconfigurations.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl describe deployments -n hotel-reservation mongodb-geo mongodb-profile mongodb-rate mongodb-recommendation mongodb-reservation mongodb-user"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 509, 'prompt_tokens': 2998, 'total_tokens': 3507, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad0-c784-7f61-9fe1-21db20b01dc5-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2998, 'output_tokens': 509, 'total_tokens': 3507}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6ed6eb20-825c-41c3-b443-1cff0fac5909'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. They used kubectl describe on several MongoDB deployments. The response from the tool call would give details about each deployment, including events, replica sets, and any errors.\n\nLooking at the previous output, the pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, which usually means the container is crashing repeatedly. The describe command should show the events associated with these deployments. Events can indicate why the pods are failing—like image pull errors, configuration issues, or resource constraints.\n\nThe user needs to check the events in the describe output. If there's an error related to the init container, that's a clue. The init container might be failing to set up the database, maybe due to incorrect credentials or missing steps. Also, checking the resource limits (CPU, memory) in the deployment specs could show if the pods are being terminated due to OOM or other resource issues.\n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB images are not compatible with the cluster's environment. The describe command might show pull errors if the image can't be fetched. Additionally, looking at the replica set status could reveal if there's a problem with the database configuration or if the pods can't communicate with each other.\n\nThe user should also check the logs of the specific pods to get more detailed error messages. But since the describe command provides a summary, starting there makes sense. If the events don't show the issue, the next step would be to check the pod logs or the init container scripts for errors.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployments -n hotel-reservation mongodb-geo mongodb-profile mongodb-rate mongodb-recommendation mongodb-reservation mongodb-user"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a7bdb421a29cb96b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 365, 'prompt_t