y replaced the variables but left the inner single quotes in the JSON structure. That\'s a problem because the JSON syntax requires double quotes for the keys and string values. Using single quotes inside the JSON string would make the JSON invalid, leading to the mongo command failing. \n\nSo the real issue is that the script has single quotes inside the JSON strings, which breaks the syntax. The solution is to ensure all quotes are properly escaped or replaced. The user needs to fix those inner single quotes in the JSON parts of the script. \n\nThe next step should be to modify the ConfigMap again, this time ensuring that all instances of single quotes within the JSON strings are replaced with double quotes or properly escaped. Then, reapply the ConfigMap and check if the pods start correctly. If that doesn\'t work, maybe there\'s another issue, like incorrect credentials or MongoDB not being ready when the script runs. But given the current information, fixing the JSON syntax is the most likely fix.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The script\'s JSON syntax still contains single quotes (e.g., `\'userAdminAnyDatabase\'`) which break MongoDB\'s command parsing. I need to fix the ConfigMap by replacing all single quotes with double quotes in the JSON strings to ensure proper syntax and variable interpolation.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml > mongo-geo-script.yaml && sed -i \'s/\\\'userAdminAnyDatabase\\\'/\\"userAdminAnyDatabase\\"/g;s/\\\'readWrite\\\'/\\"readWrite\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 578, 'prompt_tokens': 19072, 'total_tokens': 19650, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad3-961e-7a93-be34-c4a519e663c5-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 19072, 'output_tokens': 578, 'total_tokens': 19650}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='858cb95e-5d6a-4323-91a5-6d42f23a40da'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in a hotel reservation application that keep crashing. They\'ve already tried fixing the init script by changing single quotes to double quotes for variable expansion, but the pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff. \n\nLooking at the latest tool response, the ConfigMap\'s script still has single quotes around JSON keys like \'userAdminAnyDatabase\' and \'readWrite\'. MongoDB\'s shell expects double quotes for JSON syntax, so those single quotes are causing the commands to fail. Even though the variables are now properly interpolated with double quotes, the JSON syntax is incorrect because of the single quotes.\n\nThe next step should be to correct those JSON keys by replacing the single quotes with double quotes. This will ensure that the MongoDB commands are parsed correctly. The user needs to update the ConfigMap again, this time fixing the JSON syntax. After applying the corrected ConfigMap, they should check the pod status to see if the issue is resolved. If the pods start running without crashing, then the problem was indeed the JSON syntax. If not, there might be another issue, like incorrect credentials or MongoDB not starting properly, which would require further investigation.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml > mongo-geo-script.yaml && sed -i \'s/\\\'userAdminAnyDatabase\\\'/\\"userAdminAnyDatabase\\"/g;s/\\\'readWrite\\\'/\\"readWrite\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 347, 'prompt_tokens': 20248, 'total_tokens': 20595, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad3-b521-71f1-a1be-7ad6322b59fe-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 20248, 'output_tokens': 347, 'total_tokens': 20595}), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6dfa273f-be6a-4149-bbd5-e932a4c16079'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the MongoDB pods in their hotel reservation application. The problem seems to be with the init script in the ConfigMap. Earlier attempts showed that the script was using single quotes around variables, which prevented environment variable expansion. So, the variables like $ADMIN_USER weren\'t being replaced with their actual values, leading to authentication errors when MongoDB tried to create users.\n\nThe user tried modifying the ConfigMap to use double quotes for variables, but the pods are still crashing. Now, looking at the latest tool call, they replaced single quotes around \'userAdminAnyDatabase\' and \'readWrite\' with double quotes. That should fix the JSON syntax issues in the MongoDB commands. \n\nBut wait, maybe there are other instances of single quotes in the script that haven\'t been addressed. For example, in the `db.createUser` and `db.grantRolesToUser` commands, the roles are specified with single quotes. If those aren\'t fixed, the MongoDB commands will still fail because the JSON syntax is incorrect. \n\nAlso, even after fixing the quotes, the