\"readWrite\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 347, 'prompt_tokens': 20248, 'total_tokens': 20595, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad3-b521-71f1-a1be-7ad6322b59fe-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 20248, 'output_tokens': 347, 'total_tokens': 20595}), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6dfa273f-be6a-4149-bbd5-e932a4c16079'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the MongoDB pods in their hotel reservation application. The problem seems to be with the init script in the ConfigMap. Earlier attempts showed that the script was using single quotes around variables, which prevented environment variable expansion. So, the variables like $ADMIN_USER weren\'t being replaced with their actual values, leading to authentication errors when MongoDB tried to create users.\n\nThe user tried modifying the ConfigMap to use double quotes for variables, but the pods are still crashing. Now, looking at the latest tool call, they replaced single quotes around \'userAdminAnyDatabase\' and \'readWrite\' with double quotes. That should fix the JSON syntax issues in the MongoDB commands. \n\nBut wait, maybe there are other instances of single quotes in the script that haven\'t been addressed. For example, in the `db.createUser` and `db.grantRolesToUser` commands, the roles are specified with single quotes. If those aren\'t fixed, the MongoDB commands will still fail because the JSON syntax is incorrect. \n\nAlso, even after fixing the quotes, the credentials might still be incorrect. The admin and root users are using \'admin/admin\' and \'root/root\' passwords. If those credentials are wrong, the script would fail during user creation. But the user hasn\'t mentioned any password changes, so maybe the credentials are correct, or maybe the issue is with the script\'s logic.\n\nAnother angle: the init script might be running before MongoDB is fully ready. The `until mongo --eval ...` loop waits for MongoDB to start, but maybe there\'s a delay or the script is timing out. However, the logs showed MongoDB starting up, so that might not be the issue.\n\nThe user\'s last action was applying the ConfigMap with fixed quotes. Now, they need to check if the pods are running. If the pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff, the next step is to check the logs again to see if there are any new errors. If the script\'s JSON syntax is now correct, the MongoDB commands should execute without syntax errors, but authentication might still fail if the credentials are wrong.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the script is creating users that already exist, causing errors. The script has checks for existing users, but if the users are already present, it might fail. However, the initial logs didn\'t show such errors, so that\'s less likely.\n\nThe user should now check the logs of the MongoDB pods again to see if the script is executing successfully. If the logs show that the users are being created without errors, then the issue might be resolved. If not, they might need to adjust the credentials or check for other configuration issues.\n\nIn summary, the next step is to verify the pod status again and check the logs for any remaining errors. If the pods are still crashing, the logs will indicate whether the issue is with the script\'s syntax, credentials, or something else.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The MongoDB init script\'s JSON syntax still contains single quotes (e.g., `\'userAdminAnyDatabase\'`) which break MongoDB\'s command parsing. I need to reapply the ConfigMap with double quotes for all JSON keys and values to ensure proper syntax and variable interpolation.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml > mongo-geo-script.yaml && sed -i \'s/\\\'userAdminAnyDatabase\\\'/\\"userAdminAnyDatabase\\"/g;s/\\\'readWrite\\\'/\\"readWrite\\"/g;s/\\\'$ADMIN_USER\\\'/\\"$ADMIN_USER\\"/g;s/\\\'$ADMIN_PWD\\\'/\\"$ADMIN_PWD\\"/g;s/\\\'$ROOT_USER\\\'/\\"$ROOT_USER\\"/g;s/\\\'$ROOT_PWD\\\'/\\"$ROOT_PWD\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 812, 'prompt_tokens': 19372, 'total_tokens': 20184, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad3-c840-77f1-b34d-e800e2b1f0e5-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 19372, 'output_tokens': 812, 'total_tokens': 20184}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bdb4cada-0626-4ef9-bb0d-30d52250b676'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in a Kubernetes cluster that are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. They've been trying to fix the init script that's supposed to create admin and root users. \n\nFirst, they checked the deployments and pods, saw the MongoDB pods were failing. Then they looked at the deployment configs and found that the init script was using single quotes around variables, which probably prevented environment variable expansion. So the script wasn't getting the correct credentials, leading to authentication failures.\n\nThey tried editing the ConfigMap to use double quotes, but maybe the changes weren't applied correctly. The logs showed the script was still using single quotes. So they tried replacing all instances of single quotes with double quotes in the ConfigMap's script content. \n\nAfter several attempts, the user is now trying to ensure that all single quotes in the JSON parts of the script are replaced with double quotes. The last tool call was to apply the changes again. Now, they need to check if the pods are running correctly. \n\nThe next step is to verify if the MongoDB pods are now in a Running state. If they are, the issue is resolved. If not, they might need to check the logs again or look into other potential issues like resource limits or network problems. But based on the previous fixes, the main problem was the script syntax, so it's likely that after applying the correct ConfigMap, the pods should start working.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a87aa59bdcc55387', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 336, 'prompt_tokens': 20612, 'total_tokens': 20948, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad3-f32d-7d72-8916-52031d5428be-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a87aa59bdcc55387', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 20612, 'output_tokens': 336, 'total_tokens': 20948}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                     READY   STATUS             RESTARTS        AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-6s4xr            