edentials are incorrect. \n\nThe next step should be to check the init script\'s content. The ConfigMap named mongo-geo-script is mounted in the init container. By describing the ConfigMap, I can see the script\'s content. If the script is using incorrect credentials, that would cause the MongoDB container to fail authentication. \n\nSo, the plan is to check the init script\'s content to verify the credentials and any commands being executed. If the credentials are wrong, updating them in the ConfigMap would fix the issue. Alternatively, if the script is missing necessary steps, that could be the problem. \n\nI should use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to describe the ConfigMap and check the script\'s content. That way, I can identify if there\'s an issue with the init script causing the MongoDB container to fail.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The MongoDB pod logs show successful recovery but no explicit error. The issue might be with the init container script (`mongo-geo-script`) that creates admin/root users. I need to inspect the script\'s content to verify credentials or commands. Describing the ConfigMap will reveal its contents.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl describe configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 386, 'prompt_tokens': 10476, 'total_tokens': 10862, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad1-3c5e-7bf1-a37f-b6f12229c931-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10476, 'output_tokens': 386, 'total_tokens': 10862}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bacf29fb-9081-4aae-9536-ca2a6b3b7a24'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in CrashLoopBackOff状态, and they\'ve already checked the deployment and pod logs. The logs show that MongoDB starts up and recovers successfully, but the pod keeps crashing. The next step was to check the init container script because the logs didn\'t show any errors, but the pod isn\'t staying up.\n\nThe user used the `kubectl describe configmap` command to look at the `mongo-geo-script` ConfigMap, which is supposed to contain the init script for setting up the MongoDB admin user. The output shows that the script has a line `mongo admin -u admin -p admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'admin\', pwd: \'admin\', roles: [{role: \'userAdminAnyDatabase\', db: \'admin\'}]});"`. Wait, the password here is \'admin\', but maybe the script is using the wrong password or there\'s a typo. Also, the script might be failing because the MongoDB instance isn\'t ready when it runs, or there\'s an authentication issue.\n\nAnother thing to check is the `failure-script` ConfigMap, which might have error handling or retry logic. But the user hasn\'t checked that yet. Also, the MongoDB container might be exiting because the init script fails, but the logs don\'t show the error. Maybe the script is not properly handling the case where the database isn\'t ready yet. \n\nThe user should also check the `mongo-rate-script` and other similar ConfigMaps for other MongoDB services to see if they have the same issue. Additionally, looking at the events for the pod might show more details about why it\'s crashing. The user might need to check the logs of the init container specifically, or check if the script is being executed correctly. \n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB image version (8.0.14-rc0) has some issues with the init script or the way it\'s being run. The user could try updating to a stable version or checking the MongoDB documentation for any known issues. Also, ensuring that the ConfigMaps are correctly mounted and that the paths in the pod\'s volume mounts are correct. \n\nIn summary, the next step is to check the contents of the `mongo-geo-script` ConfigMap to verify the script\'s commands and credentials, then check the `failure-script` for any error handling, and possibly look into the pod\'s events and logs for more clues.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9035230cdfeccd78', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 545, 'prompt_tokens': 11594, 'total_tokens': 12139, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad1-5129-70e0-b624-8254216da129-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9035230cdfeccd78', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11594, 'output_tokens': 545, 'total_tokens': 12139}), ToolMessage(content='Name:         mongo-geo-script\nNamespace:    hotel-reservation\nLabels:       <none>\nAnnotations:  <none>\n\nData\n====\nk8s-geo-mongo.sh:\n----\n#!/bin/bash\n\n\nADMIN_USER="admin"\nADMIN_PWD="admin"\n\nROOT_USER="root"\nROOT_PWD="root"\n\nTARGET_DB="geo-db"\nREAD_WRITE_ROLE="readWrite"\n\necho "Waiting for MongoDB to start..."\nuntil mongo --eval "print(\'waited for connection\')" > /dev/null 2>&1; do\n  sleep 1\ndone\necho "MongoDB started"\n\n# Create the admin user (will fail if the user already exists)\necho "Creating admin user..."\nmongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ADMIN_USER\', pwd: \'$ADMIN_PWD\', roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"\n\n# Grant readWrite role on the target database\necho "Granting readWrite role to $ADMIN_USER on $TARGET_DB database..."\nmongo admin -u $ADMIN_USER -p $ADMIN_PWD --authenticationDatabase admin \\\n     --eval "db.grantRolesToUser(\'$ADMIN_USER\', [{role: \'$READ_WRITE_ROLE\', db: \'$TARGET_DB\'}]);"\n\n# Create the root user (will fail if the user already exists)\necho "Creating root user..."\nmongo admin --eval "db.createUser({user: \'$ROOT_USER\', pwd: \'$ROOT_PWD\', roles:[{role:\'userAdminAnyDatabase\',db:\'admin\'}]});"\n\n# Grant readWrite role on the target database to root\necho "Granting readWrite role to $ROOT_USER on $TARGET_DB database..."\nmongo admin -u $ROOT_USER -p $ROOT_PWD --authenticationDatabase admin \\\n     --eval "db.grantRolesToUser(\'$ROOT_USER\', [{role: \'$READ_WRITE_ROLE\', db: \'$TARGET_DB\'}]);"\n\n\necho "Initialization script completed"\n\n\nBinaryData\n====\n\nEvents:  <none>\n', id='57a16521-6232-42e0-b468-0c8fa56ca265', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9035230cdfeccd78'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='69b4ba4d-a0ce-4e51-a16b-084087f07411'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in CrashLoopBackOff状态. The logs show that the MongoDB service starts up, does recovery, and then seems to exit. The init script is supposed to create admin and root users, but maybe it\'s failing there.\n\nLooking at the init script from the ConfigMap, the script uses "admin/admin" and "root/root" credentials. Wait, but in MongoDB, the admin user needs to have the userAdminAnyDatabase role. The script tries to create the admin user with that role, but maybe the script is running before the MongoDB instance is fully ready, causing the commands to fail. Also, the script uses variables that might not be properly expanded. For example, in the mongo commands, the variables like $ADMIN_USER are used, but in the script, they\'re defined as strings. Wait, no, in the script, the variables are set as ADMIN_USER="admin", so when they use $ADMIN_USER in the mongo commands, that should expand correctly. But maybe the script is not being sourced properly, or there\'s an issue with the authentication.\n\nAnother thing: the script tries to create the admin user, but if the user already exists, the createUser command will fail. So maybe the script is failing because the admin user already exists, but the script doesn\'t check for that. Alternatively, the script might be trying to authenticate with the admin user before the user is created, leading to authentication errors.\n\nWait, looking at the script, the first command is to wait for MongoDB to start. Then it tries to create the admin user. But if the admin user doesn\'t exist yet, the createUser command would fail. Wait, no, the script is trying to create the admin user, which should be the first step. But maybe the script is not handling the case where the user already exists. Alternatively, the script might be trying to run the mongo commands without proper authentication, leading to errors.\n\nWait, the script uses mongo admin --eval to create the user, but that command doesn\'t require authentication unless the admin user is already created. So if the admin user doesn\'t exist, the initial mongo command might fail because it can\'t authenticate. Wait, no, the initial mongo command is connecting to the admin database without authentication. If the admin user hasn\'t been created yet, that\'s okay because the script is trying to create them. But if the script is run before the admin user is created, the first mongo command might fail. Wait, no, the script is trying to create the admin user, so the first command is to create the admin user. But maybe the script is not properly handling the authentication when it comes to the subsequent commands.\n\nWait, looking at the script, after creating the admin user, the script then uses the admin user to grant roles. But if the admin user creation fails, then the subsequent commands would fail. So the problem might be that the admin user isn\'t being created successfully, leading to the script failing. But the logs don\'t show any errors from the script. Wait, the user tried to get the logs of the previous container, but maybe the current container is still running and hasn\'t exited yet. Or maybe the script is failing silently.\n\nAlternatively, the script might be trying to run the mongo commands with the wrong authentication parameters. For example, when granting roles to the admin user, the script uses the admin user\'s credentials, but if the admin user hasn\'t been created yet, that would fail. Wait, the script first creates the admin user, then uses that user to grant roles. So the order is correct. But maybe the script is not waiting long enough for MongoDB to be ready, causing the initial connection to fail.\n\nWait, the script has a loop that waits for MongoDB to start by checking if it can run a simple command. But maybe the wait is too short, or the MongoDB instance isn\'t ready yet. Alternatively, the script is using the wrong credentials for the admin user. For example, if the admin user is supposed to have a different password, but the script is using "admin" as the password. But the user mentioned that the script uses "admin/admin" and "root/root" credentials. If those credentials are incorrect, the script would fail when trying to authenticate.\n\nWait, but the script is trying to create the admin user with those credentials. If the admin user is created with "admin/admin", then when the script tries to use that user to grant roles, it would need to authenticate. But the script is using the admin user\'s credentials in the subsequent commands. Wait, in the script, after creating the admin user, the next command is to grant roles to the admin user using the admin user\'s credentials. But if the admin user hasn\'t been created yet, that would fail. So the order is correct.\n\nAlternatively, the script might be failing because the admin user is already present, causing the createUser command to fail. But the script doesn\'t check for that. So if the admin user already exists, the createUser command would throw an error, which would cause the script to exit, leading to the container failing. But the logs don\'t show any errors from the script. Wait, the user\'s previous log output shows the script running, but maybe the error is not being captured in the logs. Or maybe the script is exiting with an error code that\'s not being logged.\n\nAnother possibility is that the script is not properly handling the authentication when connecting to MongoDB. For example, when the script runs the command to grant roles to the admin user, it\'s using the admin user\'s credentials, but if the admin user hasn\'t been created yet, that would fail. But the script first creates the admin user, so that should be okay.\n\nWait, maybe the script is not properly escaping the variables. For example, in the mongo commands, the variables like $ADMIN_USER are used, but if the script is not properly expanding them, that could cause issues. But in the script, the variables are set as strings, so they should be expanded correctly.\n\nAlternatively, the script might be failing because the MongoDB instance is not ready when the script runs. The wait loop checks if the mongo command can run, but maybe the MongoDB instance is still starting up, and the script is timing out. But the logs show that the MongoDB instance is starting up and the recovery is completed, so maybe the wait loop is successful.\n\nHmm. The user\'s logs show that the MongoDB instance is starting up and the recovery is done, but the pod is still crashing. The init script is supposed to create the admin and root users, but maybe the script is failing to do 