ser\'s credentials, but if the admin user hasn\'t been created yet, that would fail. But the script first creates the admin user, so that should be okay.\n\nHmm. I\'m a bit stuck. The logs don\'t show any errors, but the pod is crashing. The only thing I can think of is that the init script is failing to create the users, but the logs don\'t show the error. Maybe the script is not being executed, or the error is not being captured. Alternatively, the script is failing because the MongoDB instance is not ready when the script runs, even though the recovery is done.\n\nWait, the script has a wait loop that checks if the mongo command can run. If the MongoDB instance is not ready, the loop would wait. But if the loop is successful, then the script proceeds. The logs show that the loop is successful, so the script continues. Then, the script creates the admin user. If that fails, the script would exit, leading to the container crashing. But the logs don\'t show any error from the createUser command. So maybe the createUser command is succeeding, but the subsequent commands are failing.\n\nWait, the script creates the admin user and then grants roles. If the admin user is created successfully, but the grantRolesToUser command fails, that would cause the script to exit. But again, the logs don\'t show any error from that step.\n\nAlternatively, the script is not properly handling the authentication when connecting to MongoDB. For example, when the script runs the command to grant roles, it\'s using the admin user\'s credentials, but if the admin user hasn\'t been created yet, that would fail. But the script first creates the admin user, so that should be okay.\n\nI think the key issue here is that the init script is failing to create the admin and root users, but the logs don\'t show the error. The only way to confirm is to check the logs of the current container, not the previous one. The user previously tried to get the logs of the previous container, but maybe the current container\'s logs are not being captured. So the next step should be to check the logs of the current container to see if there are any errors from the init script.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The init script (`k8s-geo-mongo.sh`) is likely failing due to incorrect credentials or authentication issues. The script uses "admin/admin" and "root/root" for MongoDB, but if these credentials are invalid or the script runs before MongoDB is fully ready, it could cause the container to crash. Inspecting the current container\'s logs will reveal if the script is failing silently or encountering authentication errors.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl logs -n hotel-reservation mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 2344, 'prompt_tokens': 11073, 'total_tokens': 13417, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad1-70f8-7af3-9741-cc3c6150086e-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11073, 'output_tokens': 2344, 'total_tokens': 13417}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7539ce29-cccf-469e-af62-792a1a5c3573'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in their hotel reservation application that are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. From the previous steps, they checked the deployments, pods, and ConfigMaps. The init script for the MongoDB geo service is using "admin/admin" and "root/root" credentials. The logs from the pod show that MongoDB started successfully, but the init script might be failing.\n\nThe user tried getting the logs with `kubectl logs -n