left the inner single quotes in the JSON structure. That\'s a problem because the JSON syntax requires double quotes for the keys and string values. Using single quotes inside the JSON string would make the JSON invalid, leading to the mongo command failing. \n\nSo the real issue is that the script has single quotes inside the JSON strings, which breaks the syntax. The solution is to ensure all quotes are properly escaped or replaced. The user needs to fix those inner single quotes in the JSON parts of the script. \n\nThe next step should be to modify the ConfigMap again, this time ensuring that all instances of single quotes within the JSON strings are replaced with double quotes or properly escaped. Then, reapply the ConfigMap and check if the pods start correctly. If that doesn\'t work, maybe there\'s another issue, like incorrect credentials or MongoDB not being ready when the script runs. But given the current information, fixing the JSON syntax is the most likely fix.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The script\'s JSON syntax still contains single quotes (e.g., `\'userAdminAnyDatabase\'`) which break MongoDB\'s command parsing. I need to fix the ConfigMap by replacing all single quotes with double quotes in the JSON strings to ensure proper syntax and variable interpolation.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get configmap -n hotel-reservation mongo-geo-script -o yaml > mongo-geo-script.yaml && sed -i \'s/\\\'userAdminAnyDatabase\\\'/\\"userAdminAnyDatabase\\"/g;s/\\\'readWrite\\\'/\\"readWrite\\"/g\' mongo-geo-script.yaml && kubectl apply -f mongo-geo-script.yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 578, 'prompt_tokens': 19072, 'total_tokens': 19650, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen