131-e41a-4f1c-8a63-c18f68ea79ef'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the MongoDB pods in their hotel reservation application. They\'ve been going through several steps, like checking the deployments, pods, ConfigMaps, and logs. The main issue seems to be with the init script in the MongoDB containers. \n\nFirst, they noticed that the MongoDB pods were in CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. They checked the deployments and found that the replicas weren\'t available. Then, they looked into the pod logs and saw that the MongoDB service was starting up but failing during the init script execution. \n\nThe init script, k8s-geo-mongo.sh, was using single quotes around variables like $ADMIN_USER, which prevented environment variable expansion. That means the script wasn\'t using the correct credentials, leading to authentication failures. The user tried modifying the ConfigMap to use double quotes, but the pods still weren\'t starting properly. \n\nIn the latest tool call, they applied changes to replace single quotes with double quotes in the JSON keys and variables. However, the pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff. Now, the user needs to check if the script is actually running correctly. Maybe the script is still not properly formatted, or there\'s another issue causing the failure.\n\nThe next step should be to check the logs of the MongoDB pods again, this time looking for any errors related to the init script. If the logs show that the script is still using single quotes or there are authentication errors, the user might need to adjust the script further. Alternatively, they could check if the MongoDB users are being created correctly by connecting to the database and verifying the users. \n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB containers are not waiting long enough for the database to be ready before running the init script. The script has a loop to wait for MongoDB to start, but maybe the timeout is too short. Increasing the wait time or checking if the database is actually ready before proceeding could help. \n\nThe user could also verify the ConfigMap content again to ensure all single quotes were replaced with double quotes. If the script is correctly formatted, the next step would be to check the MongoDB authentication settings, ensuring that the admin and root users are correctly created with the right permissions. \n\nIf all else fails, restarting the MongoDB pods or scaling down and up the deployments might force the containers to re-run the init script with the corrected configuration. The user should also monitor the pod events and logs closely to pinpoint the exact cause of the crash.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely  \nreasoning: The MongoDB pods are still failing, so I need to inspect the latest logs to identify if the script is now executing correctly with double quotes or if there are new errors. This will confirm if the syntax fixes resolved the issue or if additional adjustments are needed.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl logs -n hotel-reservation mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl --tail=-1"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 606, 'prompt_tokens': 20463, 'total_tokens': 21069, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad4-0807-7fc2-93af-419c1699348e-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 20463, 'output_tokens': 606, 'total_tokens': 21069}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='425d56f7-be07-4371-9d84-3336faa62620'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with MongoDB pods in a hotel reservation application that are stuck in CrashLoopBackOff. They've tried several steps to fix the issue, including checking deployments, pods, ConfigMaps, and logs. The main problem seems to be with the init script used to create admin and root users in MongoDB. \n\nInitially, the script used single quotes around variables, which prevented environment variable expansion. The user tried replacing single quotes with double quotes, but the pods are still crashing. Now, they're checking the latest logs to see if the script is running correctly.\n\nLooking at the logs, the MongoDB service starts up, does recovery, and then the init script runs. The script attempts to create users and grant roles. However, even after fixing the quotes, there might be other issues. For example, the script might be using incorrect credentials, or the MongoDB instance might not be ready when the script runs. \n\nAnother possibility is that the script is failing during the user creation steps. The logs might show errors related to authentication or permission issues. Since the pods are still in CrashLoopBackOff, the container is exiting after the script fails. The user needs to check the exact error messages from the logs to pinpoint the issue. \n\nIf the logs show that the users are not being created, maybe the credentials are wrong, or the admin user doesn't have the necessary permissions. Alternatively, the script might be trying to connect to MongoDB before it's fully up, causing connection errors. The user should also check if the MongoDB service is properly exposed and accessible within the cluster. \n\nAnother angle is the ConfigMap itself. Even after modifying the script, there might be a mistake in how the ConfigMap is applied. The user should verify that the ConfigMap's data is correctly updated with the fixed script. \n\nIn summary, the next step is to examine the latest logs for any error messages that indicate why the script is failing. If the logs don't show errors, maybe the issue is with the MongoDB configuration or the way the init container is set up. The user might need to adjust the script further or check the MongoDB setup for any misconfigurations.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs -n hotel-reservation mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl --tail=-1"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8b0daf5110991526', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 503, 'prompt_tokens': 21590, 'total_tokens': 22093, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfad4-2905-7420-9fc5-7597307a0bf6-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs -n hotel-reservation mongodb-geo-5d9cf968b9-ztqrl --tail=-1'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8b0daf5110991526', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 21590, 'output_tokens': 503, 'total_tokens': 22093}), ToolMessage(content='{"t":{"$date":"2026-03-17T08:02:58.048+00:00"},"s":"I",  "c":"CONTROL",  "id":23285,   "ctx":"main","msg":"Automatically disabling TLS 1.0, to force-enable TLS 1.0 specify --sslDisabledProtocols \'none\'"}\n{"t":{"$date":"2026-03-17T08:02:58.049+00:00"},"s":"I",  "c":"CONTROL",  "id":5945603, "ctx":"main","msg":"Multi threading initialized"}\n{"t":{"$date":"2026-03-17T08:02:58.049+00:00"},"s":"I",  "c":"NETWORK",  "id":4648601, "ctx":"main","msg":"Implicit TCP FastOpen unavailable. If TCP FastOpen is required, set at least one of the related parameters","attr":{"relatedParameters":["tcpFastOpenServer","tcpFastOpenClient","tcpFastOpenQueueSize"]}}\n{"t":{"$date":"2026-03-17T08:02:58.051+00:00"},"s":"I",  "c":"NETWORK",  "id":4915701, "ctx":"main","msg":"Initialized wire specification","attr":{"spec":{"incomingE